            <title><![CDATA[Show HN: Run full iOS 16.5 firmware on Android Devices (293 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel</link>
            <guid>50196718</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 15:26:22 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel">https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50196718">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-hpc="true" containertiming="hpc"><article itemprop="text">
<p dir="auto"><strong>HackiOS</strong> boots Apple's open-source <strong>XNU kernel</strong> (<code>xnu-8796.121.2</code>, the kernel of iOS 16.5) on Android phone
hardware and runs the <strong>unmodified iOS 16.5 user space</strong> on top of it: launchd → backboardd → SpringBoard →
apps. Our own EFI loader, our own replacements for Apple's closed drivers, a UEFI port, and a Linux Wi-Fi driver
on a compatibility layer make that possible.</p>
<p dir="auto">It runs in two places:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>POCO X5 Pro 5G (redwood, Snapdragon 778G)</strong> — nothing is flashed: the UEFI is started with <code>fastboot boot</code>,
the whole system lives in RAM, and after a reboot the phone is back in Android;</li>
<li><strong>QEMU</strong> (<code>virt</code>, aarch64) — the same thing on any Linux computer, no phone needed.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto">This repository is half of the project:</p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Repository</th>
<th>What</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>hackios-xnu-kernel</strong> (this one)</td>
<td>XNU patches, the EFI loader, the UEFI port, Wi-Fi, QEMU scripts, <code>xl</code>, prebuilt kernel/loader/UEFI, <code>common/</code> scripts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><a href="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-userland">hackios-xnu-userland</a></td>
<td>our init, injected libraries, launchd jobs, the APFS image builder, the scripts that take iOS out of Apple's IPSW, Metal/Mesa work</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<blockquote>
<p dir="auto"><strong>No Apple files are in either repository.</strong> <code>ios165/fetch.sh</code> downloads the iOS 16.5 IPSW from Apple's own CDN
to <em>your</em> machine and extracts what is needed there. HackiOS is an independent research project by OOSEX GANG and
is not affiliated with, authorised or endorsed by Apple Inc. iOS, iPhone and Apple are trademarks of Apple Inc.</p>
</blockquote>
<p dir="auto">
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/blob/main/docs/lockscreen.jpg"><img src="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/raw/main/docs/lockscreen.jpg" width="190" alt="Lock screen"></a>
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/blob/main/docs/homescreen.jpg"><img src="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/raw/main/docs/homescreen.jpg" width="190" alt="Home screen"></a>
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/blob/main/docs/control-center.jpg"><img src="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/raw/main/docs/control-center.jpg" width="190" alt="Control Center"></a>
<a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/blob/main/docs/settings.jpg"><img src="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/raw/main/docs/settings.jpg" width="190" alt="Settings"></a>
</p>
<p dir="auto">Photographed on the phone, the Adreno GPU drawing (<code>gpu=1</code>). More in <a href="#screenshots">Screenshots</a>.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">What works</h2><a id="user-content-what-works" aria-label="Permalink: What works" href="#what-works"></a></p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Works</th>
<th>Does not work (yet)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Lock screen, swipe to unlock, home screen with all icons (two pages), stock iOS 16 wallpapers</td>
<td>Cellular, Bluetooth</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>System gestures: swipe up (home, app switcher), edge swipe (back), Control Center</td>
<td>Charging under our kernel (the phone only discharges)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Apps: Settings, Clock, Calendar, Photos, Notes, Safari and more; widgets (Weather, Calendar) on the phone</td>
<td>Metal for apps (in progress: 293 of 456 system shaders already run on the Adreno, see <code>docs/air.md</code> in hackios-xnu-userland)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Phone touchscreen (Goodix GT9916), multitouch, wake on touch; real power and volume keys</td>
<td>App Store content and Apple ID sign-in (no Secure Enclave; FairPlay daemon exits)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Up to 8 CPU cores (<code>cpus=8</code>), big core at 2.4 GHz, ~20 s to the lock screen</td>
<td>Video and Live Photo recording</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Adreno 642L GPU through Mesa/freedreno (<code>gpu=1</code>, OpenGL ES for CoreAnimation)</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Wi-Fi (WCN6750, ath11k): join from Settings, Safari over Wi-Fi, 55–90 Mbit/s</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Sound (speakers, main microphone), camera in Camera.app (front/rear, preview, focus, HEIC photos)</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Data that iOS writes survives a reboot (<code>var_store=1 ufs_write=1</code>); iOS root volume on the phone's flash</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Sileo + Procursus (dpkg, apt), tweaks, SSH</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir="auto">It is a research bench, not a ROM: a session lasts a given number of seconds, then the kernel returns to the
loader and hands over its log.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Quick start: QEMU, no kernel build</h2><a id="user-content-quick-start-qemu-no-kernel-build" aria-label="Permalink: Quick start: QEMU, no kernel build" href="#quick-start-qemu-no-kernel-build"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><code>prebuilt/</code> holds the kernel, loader and our programs built from these repositories, so all you need is the system
image, which is made on your machine from Apple's IPSW.</p>
<p dir="auto">Needs: Linux x86-64, ~12 GB free disk, 4 GB free RAM. Packages (Arch):</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="sudo pacman -S --needed git python 7zip lz4 gcc qemu-system-aarch64 qemu-ui-gtk edk2-aarch64"><pre>sudo pacman -S --needed git python 7zip lz4 gcc qemu-system-aarch64 qemu-ui-gtk edk2-aarch64</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">(Debian/Ubuntu: <code>git python3 7zip liblz4-dev gcc qemu-system-arm qemu-efi-aarch64</code>; 7-Zip must be 23 or newer — it
reads APFS.)</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="git clone https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel.git hackios
cd hackios
common/setup.sh               # brings hackios-xnu-userland into the same tree, checks the host
ios165/fetch.sh               # downloads 7.4 GB from Apple's CDN and extracts what is needed (5-15 min)
common/build.sh qemu-virt     # builds the APFS images (and the kernel, if the toolchain is there)
common/run.sh qemu-virt       # a 20-minute session in a QEMU window"><pre>git clone https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel.git hackios
<span>cd</span> hackios
common/setup.sh               <span><span>#</span> brings hackios-xnu-userland into the same tree, checks the host</span>
ios165/fetch.sh               <span><span>#</span> downloads 7.4 GB from Apple's CDN and extracts what is needed (5-15 min)</span>
common/build.sh qemu-virt     <span><span>#</span> builds the APFS images (and the kernel, if the toolchain is there)</span>
common/run.sh qemu-virt       <span><span>#</span> a 20-minute session in a QEMU window</span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">The lock screen appears after 2–3 minutes.</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Mouse with the left button held = finger.</strong> Unlock / leave an app: swipe up from the very bottom edge; back:
swipe from the left edge.</li>
<li>Keys: <code>H</code> home, <code>L</code> lock/power, <code>↑</code>/<code>↓</code> volume, <code>F11</code> (hold) five fingers.</li>
<li>From a script: <code>qemu/touch.py swipe 0.5 0.985 0.5 0.3 250</code>, <code>qemu/touch.py tap 0.615 0.71</code>,
<code>qemu/touch.py key home</code>; screenshot: <code>qemu/screendump.sh shot.png</code>.</li>
<li>Right after SpringBoard appears the system is still registering apps: give it 30–40 s.</li>
<li>Debian/Ubuntu UEFI firmware: <code>QEMU_EFI=/usr/share/AAVMF/AAVMF_CODE.fd QEMU_VARS=/usr/share/AAVMF/AAVMF_VARS.fd</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">On the phone (POCO X5 Pro 5G)</h2><a id="user-content-on-the-phone-poco-x5-pro-5g" aria-label="Permalink: On the phone (POCO X5 Pro 5G)" href="#on-the-phone-poco-x5-pro-5g"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">You need a <strong>POCO X5 Pro 5G (redwood)</strong> with an unlocked bootloader, <code>adb</code> and <code>fastboot</code>, and the images from the
section above. Nothing is written to the phone's storage.</p>
<p dir="auto"><strong>The phone's own firmware</strong> is not in the repository and cannot be: it is the vendor's and is taken from the very
phone it will run on, from its Android:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="adb pull /vendor/firmware/a660_{gmu.bin,sqe.fw,zap.mdt,zap.b00,zap.b01,zap.b02} fw-gpu/      # GPU (gpu=1)
adb pull /vendor/firmware_mnt/image/wpss.{mdt,b02,b03,b04,b05,b06,b07} fw-wifi/              # Wi-Fi (wifi=1)
adb pull /vendor/firmware_mnt/image/adsp.{mdt,b02,...,b36} fw-audio/                         # sound (adsp=1)
adb pull /vendor/firmware/aw8697_haptic.bin fw-haptic/                                       # haptics"><pre>adb pull /vendor/firmware/a660_{gmu.bin,sqe.fw,zap.mdt,zap.b00,zap.b01,zap.b02} fw-gpu/      <span><span>#</span> GPU (gpu=1)</span>
adb pull /vendor/firmware_mnt/image/wpss.{mdt,b02,b03,b04,b05,b06,b07} fw-wifi/              <span><span>#</span> Wi-Fi (wifi=1)</span>
adb pull /vendor/firmware_mnt/image/adsp.{mdt,b02,...,b36} fw-audio/                         <span><span>#</span> sound (adsp=1)</span>
adb pull /vendor/firmware/aw8697_haptic.bin fw-haptic/                                       <span><span>#</span> haptics</span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">(Put the <code>fw-*</code> directories next to the workspace. Wi-Fi also needs <code>fw-wifi/mac</code> — the radio's address on one
line — and <code>fw-wifi/network</code> — network name and password. <code>userland/build.sh</code> prints which firmware went in.)</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="common/build.sh redwood GL=1          # images + out/redwood-boot.img
common/run.sh redwood 900             # loader into RAM, system image into RAM, a 15-minute session"><pre>common/build.sh redwood GL=1          <span><span>#</span> images + out/redwood-boot.img</span>
common/run.sh redwood 900             <span><span>#</span> loader into RAM, system image into RAM, a 15-minute session</span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">or step by step with <code>xl</code>:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="./xl enter            # Android -> fastboot -> our loader in RAM (fastboot boot); it brings up its own USB link
./xl ramdisk          # upload the system image into the phone's RAM (2.5 GB, ~80 s), once per loader session
XL_SECS=900 XL_CMDLINE=&quot;touchscreen=1 cpufreq=1 bootcpu=7 cpus=8 gpu=1 wifi=1 adsp=1&quot; ./xl run
./xl screenshot shot.png
./xl reboot           # back to Android"><pre>./xl enter            <span><span>#</span> Android -&gt; fastboot -&gt; our loader in RAM (fastboot boot); it brings up its own USB link</span>
./xl ramdisk          <span><span>#</span> upload the system image into the phone's RAM (2.5 GB, ~80 s), once per loader session</span>
XL_SECS=900 XL_CMDLINE=<span><span>"</span>touchscreen=1 cpufreq=1 bootcpu=7 cpus=8 gpu=1 wifi=1 adsp=1<span>"</span></span> ./xl run
./xl screenshot shot.png
./xl reboot           <span><span>#</span> back to Android</span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto"><code>./xl</code> with no arguments lists every command (logs, screen and sound recording, camera frames, stop, state...).</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Prebuilt boot image</h2><a id="user-content-prebuilt-boot-image" aria-label="Permalink: Prebuilt boot image" href="#prebuilt-boot-image"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Every release has <code>hackios-redwood-boot.img</code> attached: the redwood UEFI with our loader inside, ready for
<code>fastboot boot</code>. It contains no Apple files; the system image is still made on your machine (above).</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Building from source</h2><a id="user-content-building-from-source" aria-label="Permalink: Building from source" href="#building-from-source"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><code>common/build.sh &lt;device&gt;</code> runs every step that this machine can run and uses <code>prebuilt/</code> for the rest.</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Kernel</strong> — needs <a href="https://theos.dev/docs/installation-linux" rel="nofollow">theos</a> in <code>~/theos</code> (cctools and
<code>iPhoneOS16.5.sdk</code>) and <code>clang lld python perl</code>. Details: <a href="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/blob/main/xnu/README.md">xnu/README.md</a>.
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="xnu/apply.sh &amp;&amp; xnu-deps/setup.sh &amp;&amp; ./xnu-build.sh -j8 -k"><pre>xnu/apply.sh <span>&amp;&amp;</span> xnu-deps/setup.sh <span>&amp;&amp;</span> ./xnu-build.sh -j8 -k</pre></div>
</li>
<li><strong>Loader</strong> — <code>xnuloader/build.sh</code> (clang + lld-link, an EFI application without EDK2).</li>
<li><strong>Images</strong> — <code>REST=1 userland/build.sh</code> (variables documented at the top of that file).</li>
<li><strong>UEFI</strong> — <a href="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/blob/main/uefi/README.md">uefi/README.md</a> (Project Silicium port); not needed to run, <code>prebuilt/uefi</code> has it.</li>
<li>After rebuilding: <code>tools/update-prebuilt.sh</code> refreshes <code>prebuilt/</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Porting to another device</h2><a id="user-content-porting-to-another-device" aria-label="Permalink: Porting to another device" href="#porting-to-another-device"></a></p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="common/new-device.sh <codename> path/to/device.dtb   # profile + loader platform from the phone's device tree"><pre>common/new-device.sh <span>&lt;</span>codename<span>&gt;</span> path/to/device.dtb   <span><span>#</span> profile + loader platform from the phone's device tree</span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Then follow <a href="https://github.com/oosexgang/hackios-xnu-kernel/blob/main/docs/PORTING.md">docs/PORTING.md</a>: the UEFI port, the loader's platform description, the kernel's
drivers, the firmware. Device profiles live in <code>common/devices/</code> (<code>redwood.conf</code>, <code>qemu-virt.conf</code>, <code>_template.conf</code>).</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">How it works</h2><a id="user-content-how-it-works" aria-label="Permalink: How it works" href="#how-it-works"></a></p>
<div data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="fastboot boot ──► UEFI (Mu-Silicium) ──► xnuloader (EFI application)
                                           │  stays resident: USB link (fastboot protocol),
                                           │  watchdog, receives the kernel's log
                                           ▼
            XNU (BCM2837 config as the base + our platform)
              ├─ GICv3, timer, video console, return to the loader
              ├─ APFS (images in RAM, iOS volume on flash), ramfs (/private/var, /tmp)
              ├─ minicrypto instead of corecrypto, AMFI and Sandbox stand-ins (MAC policies)
              ├─ driver replacements: IOSurface, IOMobileFramebuffer, AppleKeyStore, network stack
              ├─ input: /dev/redwood-touch ◄─ Goodix GT9916 (phone) / virtio-input (QEMU)
              ├─ GPU: Adreno 642L, Wi-Fi: WCN6750 (ath11k via LinuxKPI), sound: ADSP / virtio-sound
              ▼
   iOS 16.5 launchd → logd, notifyd, cfprefsd, runningboardd, backboardd, SpringBoard, apps
              ├─ our libraries are injected into backboardd, SpringBoard and mediaserverd
              │  (virtual HID services, a few ObjC method swaps — in memory only)
              └─ mediaserverd gets our CoreAudio output device (RedwoodAudio.driver)"><pre><code>fastboot boot ──► UEFI (Mu-Silicium) ──► xnuloader (EFI application)
                                           │  stays resident: USB link (fastboot protocol),
                                           │  watchdog, receives the kernel's log
                                           ▼
            XNU (BCM2837 config as the base + our platform)
              ├─ GICv3, timer, video console, return to the loader
              ├─ APFS (images in RAM, iOS volume on flash), ramfs (/private/var, /tmp)
              ├─ minicrypto instead of corecrypto, AMFI and Sandbox stand-ins (MAC policies)
              ├─ driver replacements: IOSurface, IOMobileFramebuffer, AppleKeyStore, network stack
              ├─ input: /dev/redwood-touch ◄─ Goodix GT9916 (phone) / virtio-input (QEMU)
              ├─ GPU: Adreno 642L, Wi-Fi: WCN6750 (ath11k via LinuxKPI), sound: ADSP / virtio-sound
              ▼
   iOS 16.5 launchd → logd, notifyd, cfprefsd, runningboardd, backboardd, SpringBoard, apps
              ├─ our libraries are injected into backboardd, SpringBoard and mediaserverd
