        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[What's Getting Pwned (325 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://pwn.watch/</link>
            <guid>50195543</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 14:08:12 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://pwn.watch/">https://pwn.watch/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50195543">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><main id="main"><section data-hk="00a1200070000"><p>// live feed</p><h2>What's getting pwned right now</h2><p>Ongoing security incidents, newest first: CVEs, what's affected, which versions fix it, and the advisories and posts behind each one.</p><div><dl><p><dt>live</dt><dd>36</dd></p><p><dt>critical</dt><dd>15</dd></p><p><dt title="partial fix or unpatched">not fully patched</dt><dd>18</dd></p></dl><!--$--><!--/--></div></section><section data-hk="00a1200070004" id="activity" aria-label="Incident activity"><div><h2><span>##</span> <!--$-->234<!--/--> <!--$--><!--/--><!--$-->incidents<!--/--> <!--$-->in the last 12 months<!--/--></h2></div><!--$--><p><span><span>crit 4 · high 3 · med 2 · low 1 · upd ½</span><span>severity-weighted: critical 4 · high 3 · medium 2 · low 1 · update ½</span></span><span>less<!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070005350"></span><span data-hk="00a1200070005351"></span><span data-hk="00a1200070005352"></span><span data-hk="00a1200070005353"></span><span data-hk="00a1200070005354"></span><!--/-->more</span></p><!--/--></section><div data-hk="00a12000700060"><p>Get an email when a new major security incident drops. No spam, unsubscribe any time.</p><!--$--><!--/--></div><div data-hk="00a12000700070"><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007200"><li data-hk="00a120007000720100"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201010">medium</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201020">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201030"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 22, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201050" datetime="2026-10-22T16:53:42+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 16:53 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 16:53 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201060" href="https://pwn.watch/i/us-banks-unauthorized-ai-agents-customer-accounts" link="true">Major U.S. banks detect unauthorized third-party AI agents inside customer accounts</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Online banking accounts at major U.S. banks (Morgan Stanley, Bank of America, JPMorgan Chase, Wells Fargo, Citi)<!--/--></p><p>Axios reports that major U.S. banks, including Morgan Stanley, Bank of America, JPMorgan and Wells Fargo, are detecting third-party AI agents operating inside customer accounts. In most cases the agent logs in with the customer's own credentials and permission, which breaks bank rules that require people to log in themselves. Some banks also saw agents contact call centers or take actions that appeared independent of user instructions, and executives say they often can't tell a human from an agent, or an authorized agent from a rogue one. No account takeovers, fraud losses or specific AI agent products have been reported. Morgan Stanley is blocking third-party agents and JPMorgan, Bank of America and Citi are restricting them. Don't give an AI agent your bank username and password.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a12000700072010800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a120007000720108010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201080110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/ai" link="true">AI / LLM tooling</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720108012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201080130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/jpmorgan" link="true"><span data-hk="00a120007000720108013100">#</span>JPMorgan</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720108014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201080150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/bank-of-america" link="true"><span data-hk="00a120007000720108015100">#</span>Bank of America</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720108016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201080170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/morgan-stanley" link="true"><span data-hk="00a120007000720108017100">#</span>Morgan Stanley</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720108018"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201080190" href="https://pwn.watch/t/wells-fargo" link="true"><span data-hk="00a120007000720108019100">#</span>Wells Fargo</a></li><li data-hk="00a12000700072010801a10"><a data-hk="00a12000700072010801a110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/citi" link="true"><span data-hk="00a12000700072010801a11100">#</span>Citi</a></li><li data-hk="00a12000700072010801a12"><a data-hk="00a12000700072010801a130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/banking" link="true"><span data-hk="00a12000700072010801a13100">#</span>Banking</a></li><li data-hk="00a12000700072010801a14"><a data-hk="00a12000700072010801a150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/ai-agents" link="true"><span data-hk="00a12000700072010801a15100">#</span>AI agents</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720110"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201110">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201120">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201130"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 21, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201150" datetime="2026-10-22T16:00:44+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 16:00 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 16:00 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201160" href="https://pwn.watch/i/gitlab-cve-2026-110264-regex-rce" link="true">GitLab CVSS 10 unauthenticated RCE via regex compiler (CVE-2026-110264)</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->GitLab CE/EE (self-managed)<!--/--></p><p>GitLab released a critical patch on Oct 21 2026 for CVE-2026-110264: an integer overflow in the bundled Onigmo / regex compiler that can allow an unauthenticated attacker to execute code on self-managed GitLab. CVSS 10.0; every CE/EE version from 7.0 (2014) is in range. Instances that only applied the Oct 12 regex fix remain vulnerable and must upgrade again.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072011710">CVE-2026-110264</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a12000700072011800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a120007000720118010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201180110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/devtools" link="true">Dev tools &amp; CI</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720118012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201180130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/gitlab" link="true">GitLab</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720118014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201180150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/rce" link="true">RCE</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720120"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201210">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201220">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201230"><!--$-->unpatched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 21, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201250" datetime="2026-10-22T18:15:27+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201260" href="https://pwn.watch/i/linux-kernel-zdi-reports-leaked-to-lkml" link="true">Linux kernel: unpatched ZDI bug reports with repro details leaked to public LKML (ksmbd RCE, CVSS 9.0)</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Linux kernel: ksmbd (in-kernel SMB server) and SMB Direct, skbuff/NFQUEUE networking, vmwgfx, nvmet<!--/--></p><p>On Oct 21, Trend Micro's Zero Day Initiative sent several unpatched Linux kernel vulnerability reports to security@kernel.org but Cc'd the public linux-kernel and subsystem lists, so full technical details (code paths, KASAN traces) are now public before fixes ship. The worst are two ksmbd (in-kernel SMB server) remote code execution bugs that ZDI scores CVSS 9.0: an SMB Direct integer underflow (needs an RDMA device and a writable share) and a protocol-dispatch race (needs a guest share). Also leaked: an skb_zerocopy local privilege escalation (CVSS 7.8), a vmwgfx out-of-bounds write LPE, and an nvmet info leak. No CVEs yet and no fixed mainline or stable release; the ksmbd race fix sits in the ksmbd maintainer tree and an SMB Direct fix is in a branch aimed at Linux 7.5. No exploitation reported. Only machines running ksmbd are exposed to the RCEs.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a12000700072012800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a120007000720128010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201280110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/linux" link="true">Linux</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720128012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201280130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/ksmbd" link="true">ksmbd</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720128014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201280150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/zdi" link="true">ZDI</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720128016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201280170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/rce" link="true">RCE</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720130"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201310">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201320">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201330"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 21, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201350" datetime="2026-10-22T16:00:44+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 16:00 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 16:00 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201360" href="https://pwn.watch/i/litellm-1-107-0-pypi-compromise" link="true">LiteLLM 1.107.0 PyPI/Docker supply-chain compromise</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->LiteLLM (PyPI + official Docker/GHCR images)<!--/--></p><p>On Oct 21 2026 attackers compromised LiteLLM’s release pipeline and published malicious litellm==1.107.0 to PyPI (live ~2 hours) plus compromised Docker Hub/GHCR tags (v1.107.0, latest, main-latest, main-stable). The wheels install cook.pth / cook.py / sitecustomize.py that steal cloud, developer and AI-tool credentials at every Python startup and open a remote-code loader. Second LiteLLM compromise of 2026.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072013710">MAL-2026-2144</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a12000700072013800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a120007000720138010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201380110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/python" link="true">Python</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720138012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201380130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/pypi" link="true">PyPI</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720138014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201380150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/docker" link="true">Docker</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720138016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201380170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/ai" link="true">AI / LLM tooling</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720138018"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201380190" href="https://pwn.watch/t/litellm" link="true">LiteLLM</a></li><li data-hk="00a12000700072013801a10"><a data-hk="00a12000700072013801a110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/supply-chain" link="true">Supply chain</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720140"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201410">high</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201420">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201430"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 21, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201450" datetime="2026-10-22T15:59:23+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 15:59 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 15:59 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201460" href="https://pwn.watch/i/isc-bind-cve-2026-94300" link="true">ISC BIND 9: replayed GSS-TSIG messages corrupt memory, possible RCE (CVE-2026-94300)</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->ISC BIND 9 (named) DNS server<!--/--></p><p>ISC disclosed a remotely exploitable memory-corruption bug in BIND 9's named on Oct 21. An attacker who captures a GSS-TSIG signed UDP message (the Kerberos signing used for Active Directory-style dynamic DNS updates) can replay it while the negotiated key is still valid and, combined with crafted queries to a zone they control, may get remote code execution, or at least crash named. CVSS 8.1 (High). No workaround. Affects BIND 9.11.0 through 9.18.50, 9.20.0 through 9.20.29 and 9.21.0 through 9.21.26. Fixed in 9.20.30 and 9.21.27; ISC lists no 9.18 fix. ISC knows of no active exploitation. Debian trixie has a fixed package; Debian bookworm and Ubuntu are still waiting.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072014710">CVE-2026-94300</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a12000700072014800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a120007000720148010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201480110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/linux" link="true">Linux</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720148012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201480130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/bind" link="true">BIND</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720148014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201480150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/dns" link="true">DNS</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720148016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201480170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/rce" link="true">RCE</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720150"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201510">high</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201520">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201530"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 21, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201550" datetime="2026-10-22T11:18:00+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 11:18 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 11:18 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201560" href="https://pwn.watch/i/nodejs-october-2026-security-releases" link="true">Node.js October 2026 security releases (Brotli RCE + TLS/DNS highs)</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Node.js<!--/--></p><p>On Oct 21 2026 Node.js shipped security updates for 22.x, 24.x and 26.x fixing ten vulnerabilities. The headline issue is CVE-2026-111497, a high-severity Brotli decoder out-of-bounds write that can enable remote code execution when attacker-controlled Brotli data is decoded. The same release also fixes TLS hostname/IP bypasses and DNS NUL truncation, and refreshes the bundled CA store for the eMudhra incident.</p><!--$--><div data-hk="00a1200070007201570"><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072015710">CVE-2026-111497</span><span data-hk="00a12000700072015711">CVE-2026-105482</span><span data-hk="00a12000700072015712">CVE-2026-105483</span><span data-hk="00a12000700072015713">CVE-2026-106069</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072015720">+<!--$-->6<!--/--> more</span></p><!--/--></div><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a12000700072015800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a120007000720158010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201580110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/node" link="true">Node.js</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720158012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201580130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/emudhra" link="true"><span data-hk="00a120007000720158013100">#</span>eMudhra</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720158014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201580150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/tls" link="true">TLS</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720158016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201580170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/rce" link="true">RCE</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720160"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201610">high</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201620">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201630"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 21, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201650" datetime="2026-10-22T16:32:37+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 16:32 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 16:32 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201660" href="https://pwn.watch/i/railway-env-var-security-disclosure" link="true">Railway discloses unauthorized access to customer environment variables</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Railway (hosted platform)<!--/--></p><p>On Oct 21 2026 Railway reported anomalous activity on systems storing customer environment variables. An unauthorized third party may have accessed encrypted env vars and, for some, obtained decrypted contents (API keys, DB/registry credentials). Railway says it believes this is part of a series of attacks on platform companies aimed at compromising AI inference providers, and asked upstream providers to revoke keys. Builds were paused platform-wide for ~a day; on Oct 22 they were restored for Enterprise and Pro, with Hobby/Free/Trial still pending. All users are told to rotate secrets while scoping continues.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a12000700072016800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a120007000720168010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201680110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/hosting" link="true">Cloud &amp; hosting</a></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720168012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201680130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/railway" link="true">Railway</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720170"><details><summary><time datetime="2026-10-20">Oct 20</time><!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201710">high</span><!--/--><span>EG Secure Solutions SiteGuard Server Edition: EG Secure Solutions SiteGuard Server Edition OS Command Injection Vulnerability</span><span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201720"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></span></summary><div><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->EG Secure Solutions SiteGuard Server Edition<!--/--><span> <!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201730"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span><!--/--></span></p><p>EG Secure Solutions SiteGuard Server Edition contains an OS command injection vulnerability which may allow an attacker to execute arbitrary OS commands with root or SYSTEM privileges on the system running the affected product. Added to CISA KEV on 2026-10-20.</p><div><!--$--><p><a data-hk="00a1200070007201740" href="https://pwn.watch/i/eg-secure-solutions-siteguard-server-edition-cve-2026-106931" link="true">open incident →</a></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201750"><!--$-->CVE-2026-106931<!--/--><!--$--><!--/--></span></p><!--/--></div></div></details></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720180"><details><summary><time datetime="2026-10-20">Oct 20</time><!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201810">high</span><!--/--><span>GMO Onamae Desktop Cloud malware on customer VPS</span><span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201820"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></span></summary><div><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Onamae Desktop Cloud (GMO Internet)<!--/--><span> <!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201830"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span><!--/--></span></p><p>GMO Internet’s Japanese VPS service Onamae Desktop Cloud said an attacker installed malicious programs in customer environments, forcing shutdown of as many as ~26,739 servers with no immediate restart date (mid-October 2026 reporting).</p></div></details></li><li data-hk="00a120007000720190"><details><summary><time datetime="2026-10-20">Oct 20</time><!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201910">high</span><!--/--><span>Siemens Software Center: Siemens Software Center Uncontrolled Search Path Element Vulnerability</span><span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201920"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></span></summary><div><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Siemens Software Center<!--/--><span> <!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201930"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span><!--/--></span></p><p>Siemens Software Center contains an uncontrolled search path element vulnerability that could allow an authenticated local attacker to place a specially crafted DLL in a directory that the application searches, leading to arbitrary code execution with the privileges of the affected application. Added to CISA KEV on 2026-10-20.</p><div><!--$--><p><a data-hk="00a1200070007201940" href="https://pwn.watch/i/siemens-software-center-cve-2026-110936" link="true">open incident →</a></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201950"><!--$-->CVE-2026-110936<!--/--><!--$--><!--/--></span></p><!--/--></div></div></details></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a100"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1010">unknown</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1020">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1030"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 20, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a1050" datetime="2026-10-22T11:19:00+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 11:19 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 11:19 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1060" href="https://pwn.watch/i/http3-qpack-header-accordions" link="true">HTTP/3 QPACK “Header Accordions” memory-amplification DoS</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->HTTP/3 / QPACK stacks (quic-go, Cloudflare quiche, lsquic, Bun, aioquic, h3, Wireshark, …)<!--/--></p><p>FoxIO published research (Oct 20 2026) showing that missing or late decoded-header limits in QPACK let a tiny HTTP/3 HEADERS frame expand into huge server memory use (up to ~4000× on Bun). Seven of ten implementations tested were vulnerable; quic-go, Cloudflare quiche, lsquic and Wireshark are patched, while Bun, aioquic/mitmproxy and h3 remained unpatched at publication.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a10710">CVE-2025-64702</span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a10711">CVE-2026-12523</span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a10712">CVE-2025-13945</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a10800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a108010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1080110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/go" link="true">Go</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a108012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1080130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/rust" link="true">Rust</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a108014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1080150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/python" link="true">Python</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a108016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1080170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/cloudflare" link="true">Cloudflare</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a108018"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1080190" href="https://pwn.watch/t/http3" link="true">HTTP/3</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a110"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1110">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1120">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1130"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 19, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a1150" datetime="2026-10-22T16:00:44+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 16:00 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 16:00 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1160" href="https://pwn.watch/i/opencode-mcp-oauth-rce-windows" link="true">OpenCode: malicious MCP server can run code on Windows/WSL during sign-in (no CVE)</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->OpenCode AI coding agent (Anomaly) on Windows and WSL<!--/--></p><p>A command-injection flaw in the OpenCode AI coding agent let a malicious or later-hijacked remote MCP server run PowerShell commands on a developer's Windows or WSL machine when they started the MCP OAuth sign-in. The server-supplied authorization endpoint was passed unescaped to an old version of the open package. Anomaly fixed it in 1.18.33 on Sep 28 without a CVE or advisory, so scanners won't flag 1.0.137 through 1.18.32. macOS and Linux are not affected. Pillar Security found it and rates it 9.8; no in-the-wild exploitation reported.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a11800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a118010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1180110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/ai" link="true">AI / LLM tooling</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a118012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1180130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/devtools" link="true">Dev tools &amp; CI</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a118014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1180150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/windows" link="true">Windows</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a118016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1180170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/opencode" link="true">OpenCode</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a118018"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1180190" href="https://pwn.watch/t/mcp" link="true">MCP</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a120"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1210">high</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1220">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1230"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 19, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a1250" datetime="2026-10-22T18:00:43+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 18:00 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 18:00 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1260" href="https://pwn.watch/i/capcom-id-breach-oct-2026" link="true">Capcom ID breach exposes 11.7M account email addresses</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->CAPCOM ID (Capcom online account service)<!--/--></p><p>Capcom says a third party gained unauthorized access to CAPCOM ID, the account needed to play some Capcom games online. 11,774,247 records worldwide had email addresses plus account creation time, last login time, login count and last-login IP exposed; 43 also had date of birth, gender, country/region and nickname. Passwords were changed without authorization on 196 accounts (already reset). Capcom says it has not confirmed any leak of customer-set passwords, and no credit card data was involved. Detected Oct 19; reported to Osaka Prefectural Police and Japan's PPC on Oct 21. Expect phishing impersonating Capcom.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a12800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a128010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1280110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/capcom" link="true"><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a128011100">#</span>Capcom</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a128012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1280130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/gaming" link="true"><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a128013100">#</span>Gaming</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a128014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1280150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/data-breach" link="true">Data breach</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a130"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1310">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1320">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1330"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 18, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a1350" datetime="2026-10-22T18:15:27+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1360" href="https://pwn.watch/i/emudhra-intermediate-ca-compromise" link="true">eMudhra intermediate CA private keys compromised</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->eMudhra / emSign Global TLS intermediates; any client shipping Mozilla-style CA stores<!--/--></p><p>Eight eMudhra Global TLS intermediate CA private keys were found to be cryptographically weak / recoverable (researcher report Oct 18). Mozilla, Chrome, Apple and Microsoft blocked the intermediates; eMudhra revoked them Oct 19 and the ECC root was distrusted as a precaution. Downstream runtimes that ship their own trust stores (Go, Node.js, Electron, Python certifi, curl, etc.) needed emergency CA-bundle updates so they stop trusting the compromised chains.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a13710">CVE-2026-111943</span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a13711">CVE-2026-91403</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a13800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a138010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1380110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/emudhra" link="true"><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a138011100">#</span>eMudhra</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a138012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1380130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/tls" link="true">TLS</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a140"></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a150"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1510">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1520">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1530"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 17, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a1550" datetime="2026-10-22T18:22:51+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 18:22 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 18:22 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1560" href="https://pwn.watch/i/php2shell-cve-2026-109618-109619" link="true">PHP2Shell: critical unauthenticated RCE in PHP core</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->PHP<!--/--></p><p>Two critical remote-code-execution flaws in PHP core (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619, aka “PHP2Shell”) were disclosed early after an embargo leak. Both score CVSS 9.2 and can let an unauthenticated attacker run code on servers serving PHP web requests, especially Apache mod_php. Patches shipped Oct 21 2026 (appliance vendors such as QNAP are still preparing fixes); exploitation attempts were observed before and after the patch, and watchTowr / AWS report active scanning.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a15710">CVE-2026-109618</span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a15711">CVE-2026-109619</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a15800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a158010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1580110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/php" link="true">PHP</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a158012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1580130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/apache" link="true">Apache</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a158014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1580150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/cisa-kev" link="true">CISA KEV</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a158016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1580170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/rce" link="true">RCE</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a160"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1610">high</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1620">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1630"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 17, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a1650" datetime="2026-10-22T16:32:37+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 16:32 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 16:32 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1660" href="https://pwn.watch/i/opencode-console-provider-key-breach" link="true">OpenCode Console breach exposes users' saved OpenAI / Anthropic / Google API keys</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->OpenCode Console (hosted service by Anomaly)<!--/--></p><p>On the night of Oct 16 2026 an intruder reached a legacy OpenCode Console system that was being decommissioned and may have exposed third-party AI provider keys saved in Console workspaces. OpenCode says 1.4% of Console users (those with external providers linked) were affected and tied it to a large-scale distillation attack. Users reported unauthorized charges up to ~$1,700 and some Google Cloud project suspensions. Anthropic and Google revoked/blocked exposed keys; OpenAI refunded drawn credits. The OpenCode app itself (local keys) was not affected.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a16800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a168010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1680110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/ai" link="true">AI / LLM tooling</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a168012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1680130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/opencode" link="true">OpenCode</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a168014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1680150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/data-breach" link="true">Data breach</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a170"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1710">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1720">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1730"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 16, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a1750" datetime="2026-10-22T11:20:00+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 11:20 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 11:20 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1760" href="https://pwn.watch/i/plex2shell-cve-2026-96652" link="true">Plex2Shell: chained Plex Media Server RCE exploited for miners</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Plex Media Server<!--/--></p><p>Four Plex Media Server flaws (CVE-2026-96652/96654/96655/96656), together called Plex2Shell, chain to unauthenticated admin-token theft and code execution. Researcher Zach Main published details Oct 16 and a PoC shortly after; exploitation of internet-facing servers (cryptominers + residential proxies) began by Oct 17. Fixed in 1.43.3.10861 (released earlier in August); CISA added the chain to KEV with a federal deadline of Oct 23.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a17710">CVE-2026-96652</span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a17711">CVE-2026-96654</span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a17712">CVE-2026-96655</span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a17713">CVE-2026-96656</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a17800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a178010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1780110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/plex" link="true">Plex</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a178012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1780130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/cisa-kev" link="true">CISA KEV</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a178014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a1780150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/rce" link="true">RCE</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a180"><details><summary><time datetime="2026-10-16">Oct 16</time><!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1810">high</span><!--/--><span>Fortinet Multiple Products: Fortinet Multiple Products Path Traversal Vulnerability</span><span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1820"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></span></summary><div><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Fortinet Multiple Products<!--/--><span> <!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1830"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span><!--/--></span></p><p>FortiOS, FortiPAM, FortiProxy, and FortiSwitchManager contain a path traversal vulnerability that may allow an authenticated attacker with admin profile and at least read-write permissions to write or delete arbitrary files via specific CLI commands. Added to CISA KEV on 2026-10-16.</p></div></details></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a190"><details><summary><time datetime="2026-10-15">Oct 15</time><!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1910">high</span><!--/--><span>Atlassian Multiple Products: Atlassian Multiple Products Files or Directories Accessible to External Parties Vulnerability</span><span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1920"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></span></summary><div><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Atlassian Multiple Products<!--/--><span> <!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a1930"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span><!--/--></span></p><p>Multiple Atlassian products (Bitbucket Data Center ,Confluence Data Center, Jira Service Management Data Center, Jira Software Data Center, Bamboo Data Center, Crowd Data Center, Crucible and Fisheye) contain a files or directories accessible to external parties vulnerability which could allow an unauthenticated attacker to access specific files within the web application root directory in affected versions. Added to CISA KEV on 2026-10-15.</p></div></details></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a200"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2010">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2020">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2030"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 14, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a2050" datetime="2026-10-22T18:00:43+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 18:00 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 18:00 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2060" href="https://pwn.watch/i/nextjs-october-2026-rce-security-release" link="true">Next.js out-of-band release fixes two unauthenticated RCEs (SVG image optimization CVE-2026-96889, use cache)</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Next.js (next on npm), sharp / librsvg<!--/--></p><p>On Oct 14 2026 Vercel shipped Next.js 16.4.1 and 15.5.28 with two critical unauthenticated remote code execution fixes held back from the September release: CVE-2026-96889 / GHSA-2464-fhr4-w9xw, a use-after-free in librsvg (via sharp) reachable through the Image Optimization API when SVGs are processed on glibc-based Linux, and GHSA-673q-mjm6-r5xr, RCE in App Router apps with Cache Components when a `use cache` function returns multiple typed arrays. A high-severity Server Actions DoS (GHSA-p3m5-59mp-jv5r) is fixed too. GitHub added the SVG advisory to its global database on Oct 22. No known exploitation.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a20710">CVE-2026-96889</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a20800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a208010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2080110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/node" link="true">Node.js</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a208012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2080130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/npm" link="true">npm</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a208014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2080150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/nextjs" link="true">Next.js</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a208016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2080170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/vercel" link="true">Vercel</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a208018"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2080190" href="https://pwn.watch/t/sharp" link="true"><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a208019100">#</span>sharp</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a20801a10"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a20801a110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/rce" link="true">RCE</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a210"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2110">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2120">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2130"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 14, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a2150" datetime="2026-10-22T18:15:27+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2160" href="https://pwn.watch/i/zeabur-october-2026-breach-shutdown" link="true">Zeabur breach exposes 3.2M env-var records; company to shut down</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Zeabur (PaaS)<!--/--></p><p>Zeabur disclosed an October 14–15 2026 security incident in which an attacker used leaked internal credentials stored in plaintext to access shared clusters in 9 regions, root into 101 Zeabur-managed GCP dedicated servers, and read the platform database, exposing ~3.22 million environment-variable records across 321,810 accounts. Zeabur is winding down: from December 1 2026 shared-cluster services pause and dedicated servers stop renewing, and on December 31 all services stop and service data is deleted. Refund and incident-claim requests close November 30. Users must rotate every secret stored on Zeabur and migrate off.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a21800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a218010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2180110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/hosting" link="true">Cloud &amp; hosting</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a218012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2180130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/zeabur" link="true"><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a218013100">#</span>Zeabur</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a218014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2180150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/data-breach" link="true">Data breach</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a220"><details><summary><time datetime="2026-10-14">Oct 14</time><!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2210">high</span><!--/--><span>Ahsay AhsayCBS: Ahsay AhsayCBS Command Injection Vulnerability</span><span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2220"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></span></summary><div><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Ahsay AhsayCBS<!--/--><span> <!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2230"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span><!--/--></span></p><p>Ahsay AhsayCBS contains a command injection vulnerability that could allow a remote attacker to execute operating system commands via manipulation of the random parameter in the Replication Receiver component’s /rps/api/json/UpdateReceivers.do endpoint. This vulnerability can be chained with CVE-2026-105133. CISA added CVE-2026-105134, CVE-2026-105133 to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on 2026-10-14 based on evidence of active exploitation.</p><div><!--$--><p><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2240" href="https://pwn.watch/i/ahsay-ahsaycbs-cve-2026-105134" link="true">open incident →</a></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2250"><!--$-->CVE-2026-105134 · CVE-2026-105133<!--/--><!--$--><!--/--></span></p><!--/--></div></div></details></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a230"><details><summary><time datetime="2026-10-14">Oct 14</time><!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2310">high</span><!--/--><span>Cisco Secure Email Gateway: Cisco Secure Email Gateway Relative Path Traversal Vulnerability</span><span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2320"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></span></summary><div><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Cisco Secure Email Gateway<!--/--><span> <!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2330"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span><!--/--></span></p><p>Cisco Secure Email Gateway contains a relative path traversal vulnerability that could allow an unauthenticated, remote attacker to write arbitrary files on the underlying operating system. Added to CISA KEV on 2026-10-14.</p></div></details></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a240"><details><summary><time datetime="2026-10-14">Oct 14</time><!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2410">high</span><!--/--><span>Palo Alto Networks PAN-OS: Palo Alto Networks PAN-OS Code Injection Vulnerability</span><span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2420"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></span></summary><div><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Palo Alto Networks PAN-OS<!--/--><span> <!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2430"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span><!--/--></span></p><p>Palo Alto Networks PAN-OS contains a code injection vulnerability that could allow an authenticated attacker with network access to execute arbitrary code with root privileges on the firewall. Added to CISA KEV on 2026-10-14.</p></div></details></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a250"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2510">medium</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2520">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2530"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 14, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a2550" datetime="2026-10-22T14:58:57+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 14:58 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 14:58 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2560" href="https://pwn.watch/i/godaddy-mass-certificate-revocation-oct-2026" link="true">GoDaddy mass-revokes 404,607 TLS certificates over CP/CPS profile mismatch</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->GoDaddy / Starfield TLS certificates (DV, OV, EV)<!--/--></p><p>GoDaddy (Starfield) found that 404,607 TLS certificates carried policy information (policy OIDs, CPS URI) that didn't match its own published CP/CPS, which under the CA/Browser Forum Baseline Requirements means they must be revoked within five days. GoDaddy missed the Oct 18 deadline and says the certificates were revoked on Oct 21 2026. Nothing was hacked and no keys leaked, but sites still serving a revoked certificate can show browser warnings or become unreachable until the re-issued certificate is installed.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a25800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a258010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2580110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/hosting" link="true">Cloud &amp; hosting</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a258012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2580130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/godaddy" link="true">GoDaddy</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a258014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2580150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/tls" link="true">TLS</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a260"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2610">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2620">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2630"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 8, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a2650" datetime="2026-10-22T11:18:00+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 11:18 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 11:18 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2660" href="https://pwn.watch/i/pwnescape-cve-2026-99999" link="true">PwnEscape: Linux networking guest-to-host escape (CVE-2026-99999)</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Linux kernel networking (host side of KVM/Firecracker and similar)<!--/--></p><p>Paulos Yibelo / pwn.ai found a Critical Linux networking flaw (CVE-2026-99999, CVSS 9.3) during Vercel’s $1M Sandbox challenge. Guest-controlled checksum metadata can rewrite an IPv4 header after validation, enabling host-memory disclosure and, in lab conditions, guest-to-host code execution on KVM/Firecracker. Upstream fix merged Oct 2026; host kernels need patch + reboot. Affects multi-tenant VPS / sandbox providers more than end-user desktops.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a26710">CVE-2026-99999</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a26800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a268010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2680110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/linux" link="true">Linux</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a268012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2680130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/vercel" link="true">Vercel</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a268014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2680150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/kvm" link="true">KVM</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a268016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2680170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/firecracker" link="true">Firecracker</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a270"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2710">high</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2720">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2730"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 8, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a2750" datetime="2026-10-22T12:04:00+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 12:04 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 12:04 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2760" href="https://pwn.watch/i/ghostaction-github-workflows-oct-2026" link="true">GhostAction returns: hijacked GitHub accounts push secret-stealing 'security audit' workflows to 30k+ repos</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->GitHub repositories / GitHub Actions (compromised maintainer accounts)<!--/--></p><p>GhostAction, a campaign first documented by GitGuardian in Sept 2025, came back in 2026. It hit 772 repos in Aug-Sep 2026, then surged on Oct 8-12: about 1,900 compromised GitHub accounts pushed fake 'security audit' workflows to 30,000+ repositories (GitGuardian). The new payload exfiltrates the repo's Actions secrets plus every credential found in the full git history to 193.32.204.199 over plain HTTP. GitHub began preventing the workflow from running around Oct 9. The operator moved on to GitHub-themed phishing (my-github.com) and a VS Code tasks.json + fake .woff2 font stealer found in 94 repos.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a27800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a278010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2780110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/github-actions" link="true">GitHub Actions</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a278012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2780130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/devtools" link="true">Dev tools &amp; CI</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a278014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2780150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/github" link="true">GitHub</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a278016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2780170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/vscode" link="true"><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a278017100">#</span>VS Code</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a280"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2810">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2820">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2830"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 7, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a2850" datetime="2026-10-22T18:15:27+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2860" href="https://pwn.watch/i/tensorlake-npm-shai-hulud-worm" link="true">tensorlake@0.5.144 npm Shai-Hulud worm with hostage GitHub token</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->tensorlake (npm)<!--/--></p><p>On Oct 8 2026, tensorlake@0.5.144 was published from the real GitHub repo (with npm provenance) carrying a preinstall hook that installs a Shai-Hulud-style credential-stealing worm. It harvests npm/GitHub/cloud/SSH/AI-tool secrets, spreads to other packages, and installs a “gh-token-monitor” that wipes the home directory if the stolen GitHub token is revoked. Pin 0.5.143; remove the monitor before rotating tokens.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a28710">GHSA-8g63-53c9-65j2</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a28800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a288010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2880110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/node" link="true">Node.js</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a288012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2880130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/npm" link="true">npm</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a288014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2880150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/github" link="true">GitHub</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a288016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2880170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/shai-hulud" link="true">Shai-Hulud</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a288018"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a2880190" href="https://pwn.watch/t/supply-chain" link="true">Supply chain</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a290"><details><summary><time datetime="2026-10-04">Oct 4</time><!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2910">high</span><!--/--><span>Citrix NetScaler: Citrix NetScaler Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer Vulnerability</span><span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2920"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></span></summary><div><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Citrix NetScaler<!--/--><span> <!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a2930"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span><!--/--></span></p><p>Citrix NetScaler ADC (formerly Citrix ADC) and Citrix NetScaler Gateway (formerly Citrix Gateway) contain an improper restriction of operations within the bounds of a memory buffer vulnerability that could allow for a denial of service. Added to CISA KEV on 2026-10-04.</p></div></details></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a300"><details><summary><time datetime="2026-10-02">Oct 2</time><!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a3010">high</span><!--/--><span>Zammad GmbH Zammad: Zammad GmbH Zammad Improper Privilege Management Vulnerability</span><span><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a3020"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></span></summary><div><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Zammad GmbH Zammad<!--/--><span> <!--$--><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a3030"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span><!--/--></span></p><p>Zammad GmbH Zammad contains an improper privilege management vulnerability that can allow the local zammad user to escalate privileges to root. This vulnerability can be chained with CVE-2026-102489. CISA added CVE-2026-102490, CVE-2026-102489 to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on 2026-10-02 based on evidence of active exploitation.</p><div><!--$--><p><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a3040" href="https://pwn.watch/i/zammad-gmbh-zammad-cve-2026-102490" link="true">open incident →</a></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a3050"><!--$-->CVE-2026-102490 · CVE-2026-102489<!--/--><!--$--><!--/--></span></p><!--/--></div></div></details></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a310"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a3110">high</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a3120">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a3130"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Oct 1, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a3150" datetime="2026-10-22T11:20:00+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 11:20 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 11:20 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a3160" href="https://pwn.watch/i/ivanti-connect-secure-cve-2026-104935" link="true">Ivanti Connect Secure zero-day chain (CVE-2026-104935) exploited for root</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Ivanti Connect Secure (ICS)<!--/--></p><p>Volexity observed threat actor UTA0576 chaining an Ivanti Connect Secure authentication-bypass / RCE zero-day (CVE-2026-104935) with additional flaws from about Oct 1–3 2026 to get unauthenticated root on fully patched appliances, re-exploiting in memory without planting webshells. Ivanti patched CVE-2026-104935 on Oct 13; CISA added it to KEV. One privilege-escalation piece of the chain was still being fixed at disclosure.</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a31710">CVE-2026-104935</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a31800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a318010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a3180110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/ivanti" link="true"><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a318011100">#</span>Ivanti</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a318012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a3180130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/zero-day" link="true">Zero-day</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a318014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a3180150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/cisa-kev" link="true">CISA KEV</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a318016"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a3180170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/rce" link="true">RCE</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a320"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a3210">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a3220">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a3230"><!--$-->patched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Sep 30, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200070007201a3250" datetime="2026-10-22T11:18:00+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 11:18 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 11:18 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a3260" href="https://pwn.watch/i/fortimail-cve-2026-104286" link="true">FortiMail unauthenticated file-write zero-day CVE-2026-104286</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Fortinet FortiMail<!--/--></p><p>Fortinet FortiMail CVE-2026-104286 (CVSS 9.8) is an unauthenticated arbitrary file write via path traversal / null-byte issues in Identity-Based Encryption web flows. CISA confirmed active exploitation and ordered federal mitigation; Fortinet published FG-IR-26-175 with patches and interim mitigations (disable IBE / restrict admin/webmail).</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a1200070007201a32710">CVE-2026-104286</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a1200070007201a32800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a328010"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a3280110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/fortinet" link="true">Fortinet</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a328012"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a3280130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/zero-day" link="true">Zero-day</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007201a328014"><a data-hk="00a1200070007201a3280150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/cisa-kev" link="true">CISA KEV</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li></ul><details data-hk="00a1200070007210"><summary><span>show <!--$-->3<!--/--> <!--$-->more live<!--/--> <!--$-->incidents<!--/--></span><span><!--$-->3<!--/--> <!--$-->more live<!--/--> <!--$-->incidents<!--/--></span></summary><!--$--><section data-hk="00a12000700072110" aria-label="September 2026"><h3><!--$-->September 2026<!--/--> <span>1</span></h3><ul><li data-hk="00a1200070007211100"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072111010">high</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072111020">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072111030"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Sep 9, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a12000700072111050" datetime="2026-10-22T11:20:00+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 11:20 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 11:20 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a12000700072111060" href="https://pwn.watch/i/pingcap-tidb-cloud-breach" link="true">PingCAP / TiDB Cloud breach: stolen service-account credentials, data and source code taken</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->PingCAP TiDB Cloud (hosted database platform)<!--/--></p><p>PingCAP confirmed that between Sep 9 and Oct 7 2026 an unauthorized party used stolen service-account credentials to access internal systems, copy data and obtain source code. Exported records included TiDB Cloud user names/emails, org/project associations, SQL-editor text, billing/contract data and an encrypted API-key table. Extortion group FulcrumSec claims 10.6 TB and 115k plaintext API keys; those figures are unverified and PingCAP disputes the API-key claim. No evidence data stored in TiDB was affected.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a120007000721110800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007211108010"><a data-hk="00a12000700072111080110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/hosting" link="true">Cloud &amp; hosting</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007211108012"><a data-hk="00a12000700072111080130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/pingcap" link="true">PingCAP</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007211108014"><a data-hk="00a12000700072111080150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/tidb" link="true">TiDB</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007211108016"><a data-hk="00a12000700072111080170" href="https://pwn.watch/t/data-breach" link="true">Data breach</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li></ul></section><section data-hk="00a12000700072112" aria-label="August 2026"><h3><!--$-->August 2026<!--/--> <span>1</span></h3><ul><li data-hk="00a1200070007211300"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072113010">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072113020">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072113030"><!--$-->partial fix<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Aug 29, 2026</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a12000700072113050" datetime="2026-10-22T18:15:27+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a12000700072113060" href="https://pwn.watch/i/beyondtrust-remote-support-cve-2026-102764" link="true">BeyondTrust Remote Support zero-day CVE-2026-102764 (ARMORY CASTLE)</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->BeyondTrust Remote Support (formerly Bomgar)<!--/--></p><p>China-nexus actor ARMORY CASTLE (ex-UNC5221) exploited CVE-2026-102764, a critical unauthenticated file-operations flaw in BeyondTrust Remote Support, as a zero-day from late August 2026. BeyondTrust issued BT26-05-RS; SaaS was auto-patched by Oct 9; CISA added the CVE to KEV on Oct 16. Self-hosted appliances need the patch plus forensics — patching alone does not clear existing footholds (SAWFLY / BARECLAW).</p><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a120007000721130710">CVE-2026-102764</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a120007000721130800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007211308010"><a data-hk="00a12000700072113080110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/beyondtrust" link="true"><span data-hk="00a1200070007211308011100">#</span>BeyondTrust</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007211308012"><a data-hk="00a12000700072113080130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/zero-day" link="true">Zero-day</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007211308014"><a data-hk="00a12000700072113080150" href="https://pwn.watch/t/cisa-kev" link="true">CISA KEV</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li></ul></section><section data-hk="00a12000700072114" aria-label="February 2025"><h3><!--$-->February 2025<!--/--> <span>1</span></h3><ul><li data-hk="00a1200070007211500"><div><div><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072115010">critical</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072115020">live</span></p><!--/--><!--$--><p><span data-hk="00a12000700072115030"><!--$-->unpatched<!--/--></span></p><!--/--><!--$--><!--/--><p><span><span>since Feb 19, 2025</span><!--$--><time data-hk="00a12000700072115050" datetime="2026-10-22T18:15:27+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC"><!--$-->updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 18:15 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></span></p></div><h2><a data-hk="00a12000700072115060" href="https://pwn.watch/i/fearless-wallet-extension-key-leak" link="true">Fearless Wallet browser extension leaked private keys for ~20 months</a></h2><p><span>›</span> <!--$-->Fearless Wallet browser extension (Chrome; Firefox build delisted)<!--/--></p><p>The Polkadot Community Foundation warned that Fearless Wallet browser-extension versions 3.0.1, 3.0.3, 3.0.4 and 3.0.5 contained code, added Feb 19 2025 inside a large 'TON ecosystem' change, that sent private keys/recovery data to an attacker server when the wallet was unlocked. The code was active at different times until Oct 10 2026. Eight users have reported losses totalling 17,286 DOT. Anyone who used the extension since Feb 2025 must create a new keypair in another wallet and move funds.</p><!--$--><!--/--><!--$--><ul data-hk="00a120007000721150800" aria-label="Tags"><li data-hk="00a1200070007211508010"><a data-hk="00a12000700072115080110" href="https://pwn.watch/t/chrome" link="true">Chrome</a></li><li data-hk="00a1200070007211508012"><a data-hk="00a12000700072115080130" href="https://pwn.watch/t/data-breach" link="true">Data breach</a></li></ul><!--/--></div></li></ul></section><!--/--></details><!--/--></div></main></div><div><p>Not security advice. Always check the vendor advisory before acting.</p><p><a href="https://pwn.watch/feed.xml" rel="external">rss</a> · <a href="https://pwn.watch/incidents.json" rel="external">json</a> · <a href="https://pwn.watch/llms.txt" rel="external">llms.txt</a> · <!--$--><time data-hk="00a1200090" datetime="2026-10-22T18:22:51+02:00" title="Oct 22, 2026, 18:22 UTC"><!--$-->last updated <!--/--><!--$-->Oct 22, 2026, 18:22 UTC<!--/--></time><!--/--></p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[eMudhra to Exit Global SSL/TLS Certificate Business (896 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://emudhra.com/en/press-release/emudhra-to-exit-global-ssl-tls-certificate-business</link>
            <guid>50195110</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 13:33:39 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://emudhra.com/en/press-release/emudhra-to-exit-global-ssl-tls-certificate-business">https://emudhra.com/en/press-release/emudhra-to-exit-global-ssl-tls-certificate-business</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50195110">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><p><i>Will Sharpen Focus on Core Enterprise Solutions and trust services other than SSL/TLS</i></p> <p>BENGALURU, INDIA, October 22, 2026 - <a href="https://emudhra.com/en/">eMudhra</a> Limited (BSE: 543533 | NSE: EMUDHRA), a global digital trust company, today announced that its Board has decided to voluntarily withdraw from the global SSL/TLS certificate business.</p> <p>Mr. V. Srinivasan, Chairman, eMudhra Limited referred to the recent incident relating to its global SSL/TLS certificate and indicated that eMudhra handled the recent incident diligently, revoking the affected CAs within 14 hours of discovery and disclosing promptly. There were no end entity certificates issued out of the affected CA’s. Its infrastructure was not compromised and no data was breached. All physical and logical security controls remained intact.</p> <p>He added that global SSL/TLS is a small part of eMudhra’s business contributing to about 1% of the global revenue. Due to increasing data sovereignty &amp; compliance requirements, complexity and cost associated with this business is increasing over the years. This, paired with the fact that some of the large entities issue the SSL/TLS free of cost, the business potential and RoI of this line of business also has considerably reduced for eMudhra when measured against our cumulative revenue base.</p> <p>However, the level of social media amplification around this incident has brought scrutiny that is out of proportion to SSL/TLS’s place in our business. In view of this, after detailed deliberations our board has concluded that withdrawing from global SSL/TLS is the right decision for eMudhra and its shareholders. For many certifying authorities, SSL/TLS is the whole business. For eMudhra it is one part of a much broader digital solutions portfolio. We will put our energy where we create the most value for customers with Sharpened Focus on Core Enterprise Solutions and trust services other than SSL/TLS.</p> <p><strong>Seamless transition for customers</strong></p> <p>Our G1 RSA roots will continue to serve customers until this transition is complete as we work with browser programs and our customers to enable this transition. eMudhra will reach out to affected customers to replace any certificate free of cost from partners through the transition. Revocation and certificate status services will keep running until every issued certificate has expired or been replaced. eMudhra will complete its remaining commitments to the browser root programs, including a full root-cause report.</p> <p><strong>Core businesses unaffected</strong></p> <p>eMudhra’s global SSL/TLS infrastructure runs separately from its other operations. The company’s Global digital signature certificate (DSC), eSign, and other signing services, including those in India, its enterprise PKI Solutions (emCA, CertiNext platforms), emSigner eSignature Suite, and its identity and authentication solutions (SecurePass, emAS) are not affected and continue to operate as normal.</p> <p><strong>About eMudhra</strong></p> <p>With over 17 years of expertise, eMudhra is a global provider of digital identity, trust, and security solutions, enabling secure digital transformation for banks and financial institutions (BFSI), enterprises, and governments across more than 35 countries worldwide. Its product suite spans CertiNext (PKI and certificate lifecycle management), SecurePass (identity and access management, multi-factor authentication and converged identity), and PrivaTrust (data discovery and consent management for global data-privacy compliance and DSARs), delivering high-assurance digital trust at population scale.</p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Crystal 1.22 (167 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://crystal-lang.org/2026/10/12/1.22.0-released/</link>
            <guid>50194938</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 13:20:13 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://crystal-lang.org/2026/10/12/1.22.0-released/">https://crystal-lang.org/2026/10/12/1.22.0-released/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50194938">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div itemprop="articleBody">


<div>
  <h2>Highlights</h2>

  <ul>
<li>
    <span>
      <span><a href="#breaking-exit-on-pipe-error">Standard streams emulate SIGPIPE</a>
</span>
      <span>stdlib/runtime</span>
    </span>
  </li>
<li>
    <span>
      <span><a href="#dwarf-refactor">Revamped DWARF parsing for performance</a>
</span>
      <span>stdlib/runtime</span>
    </span>
  </li>
<li>
    <span>
      <span><a href="#eventloop">All targets support native event loop</a>
</span>
      <span>stdlib/runtime</span>
    </span>
  </li>
<li>
    <span>
      <span><a href="#dependencies">Support for LLVM 23 &amp; 24</a>
</span>
      <span>stdlib/llvm</span>
    </span>
  </li>
<li>
    <span>
      <span><a href="#aarch64-linux-promoted-to-tier-1"><code>aarch64-linux</code> promoted to Tier 1</a>
</span>
      <span>platform/linux</span>
    </span>
  </li>

</ul>

</div>


<p>We are announcing a new Crystal release 1.22.0 with several new features and bug
fixes.</p>

<p>Pre-built packages are available on <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/releases/tag/1.22.0">GitHub
Releases</a> and our
official distribution channels. See
<a href="https://crystal-lang.org/install/">crystal-lang.org/install</a> for installation
instructions.</p>



<p>This release includes <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/pulls?q=is%3Apr+milestone%3A1.22.0">251 changes since
1.21.1</a>
by 33 contributors. We thank all the contributors for all the effort put into
improving the language! ❤️</p>



<p>Below we list the most remarkable changes in the language, compiler, and stdlib.
For more details, visit the <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/blob/master/doc/changelogs/v1.22.md">full
changelog</a>.</p>



<p>Crystal processes exit immediately when writing to STDOUT or STDERR fails with <code>EPIPE</code>.
This closely resembles the default behaviour of the <code>SIGPIPE</code> signal.
Previously, the failing write would raise <code>IO::Error</code>, which typically prints
an error message and exits. This error message is now removed (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17335">#17335</a>).</p>

<p>You can opt out of this behaviour with <code>STDOUT.exit_on_broken_pipe = false</code>.</p>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/straight-shoota">@straight-shoota</a></em></p>



<p>Constants can have type declarations (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/16951">#16951</a>).
They work exactly as you would expect the combination of constants and type declarations to be.</p>

<div><pre><code><span>module</span> <span>Foo</span>
  <span>BAR</span> <span>:</span> <span>Int32</span> <span>=</span> <span>1</span>
  <span>BAZ</span> <span>:</span> <span>String</span> <span>=</span> <span>""</span>
  <span>QUX</span> <span>:</span> <span>Int64</span> <span>=</span> <span>123</span> <span># autocasts 123 to Int64</span>
<span>end</span>
</code></pre></div>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/stakach">@stakach</a></em></p>



<p>The targets <code>aarch64-linux-gnu</code> and <code>aarch64-linux-musl</code> moved from Tier 2 to Tier 1.
The only missing piece was that we were lacking official builds, but we have that since 1.19.</p>



<p><code>Process.fork</code> had been deprecated sine before 1.0 and now we finally removed it (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17367">#17367</a>)</p>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/straight-shoota">@straight-shoota</a></em></p>



<ul>
  <li>New macros enable more text operations at compile time: <code>StringLiteral#bytesize</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17176">#17176</a>), <code>NumberLiteral#chr</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17169">#17169</a>) and <code>RangeLiteral#each</code> supports <code>CharLiteral</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17170">#17170</a>)</li>
</ul>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/straight-shoota">@straight-shoota</a></em></p>



<p>New constants <code>Crystal::ALL_FLAGS</code> and <code>Crystal::USER_FLAGS</code> provide access to the compile time flags (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17392">#17392</a>).</p>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/straight-shoota">@straight-shoota</a></em></p>



<p>We completely revamped DWARF parsing to enhance performance and reduce memory usage.
The DWARF parser now scans sections on demand (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17283">#17283</a>) and builds an efficient index table once (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17410">#17410</a>, <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17455">#17455</a>), which results in blazingly fast look ups.
Decoded backtrace lines are cached in an LRU cache (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17285">#17285</a>).</p>

<p>Also added aupport for DWARF 5 features <code>DW_FORM_addrx&lt;n&gt;</code> and <code>DW_FORM_strx&lt;n&gt;</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17445">#17445</a>).</p>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/ysbaddaden">@ysbaddaden</a></em></p>



<p>We merged numerous improvements and bug fixes for HTTP.</p>

<ul>
  <li><code>#remote_address</code> and <code>#local_address</code> moved from <code>HTTP::Request</code> to <code>HTTP::Context</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17196">#17196</a>)</li>
  <li>Add <code>HTTP::Headers#merge</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17326">#17326</a>)</li>
  <li>Serialize <code>IO::Memory</code> in HTTP body with <code>Content-Length</code> header (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17388">#17388</a>)</li>
</ul>

<p>Bug fixes:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Reject unrecognized HTTP <code>Transfer-Encoding</code> header (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17140">#17140</a>), invalid <code>Content-Encoding</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17262">#17262</a>), and invalid HTTP trailer (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17337">#17337</a>)</li>
  <li><strong>[breaking]</strong> Fix the return values of <code>HTTP::Request#body=</code> and <code>#content_length=</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17257">#17257</a>, <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17273">#17273</a>)</li>
  <li>Fix <code>HTTP.parse_headers_and_body</code> to not raise with <code>without_zlib</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17272">#17272</a>)</li>
  <li>Validate properties of <code>HTTP::WebSocketHandler</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17235">#17235</a>), <code>HTTP::Request</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17255">#17255</a>,<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17304">#17304</a>, <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17374">#17374</a>, <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17310">#17310</a>, <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17312">#17312</a>, <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17258">#17258</a>), <code>HTTP::Headers</code> name (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17317">#17317</a>)</li>
  <li>Close <code>HTTP::Client</code> on <code>IO::Error</code> during request (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17318">#17318</a>)</li>
  <li>Fix <code>Content-Encoding: deflate</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17261">#17261</a>)</li>
  <li>Fix <code>HTTP::Request.from_io</code> parsing empty header name (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17254">#17254</a>)</li>
  <li>Fix <code>HTTP::Request#form_params?</code> to ignore invalid content-type (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17256">#17256</a>)</li>
</ul>

<p><em>Thanks <a href="https://github.com/straight-shoota">@straight-shoota</a>, <a href="https://github.com/hahwul">@hahwul</a>, <a href="https://github.com/jgaskins">@jgaskins</a>, <a href="https://github.com/Sija">@Sija</a></em></p>



<p>All targets support a native event loop based directly on the system selector and use it by default.</p>

<p>We enabled <code>epoll</code> on Solaris (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17370">#17370</a>) and <code>kqueue</code> on all remaining BSD targets (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17421">#17421</a>, <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17420">#17420</a>).
<code>libevent</code> is still available as a fallback, but now it’s strictly opt-in and becoming technically obsolete. We expect to deprecate in a following release.</p>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/ysbaddaden">@ysbaddaden</a></em></p>



<p>We finally enabled support for Kernel TLS in the OpenSSL bindings by default (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17059">#17059</a>)</p>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/ysbaddaden">@ysbaddaden</a></em></p>



<ul>
  <li><code>File.exists?</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17307">#17307</a>), as well as <code>File::Info.readable?</code>, <code>.writable?</code>, <code>.executable?</code> now receive a <code>follow_symlinks: false</code> argument(<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17391">#17391</a>)</li>
  <li>Add <code>Char::Reader.new(Bytes)</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17428">#17428</a>)</li>
  <li>Add <code>Time::Span#to_nanoseconds</code> etc. (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17190">#17190</a>)</li>
  <li>Add <code>GC.syscall(&amp;)</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17150">#17150</a>)</li>
  <li>Fixed backtraces when linking with <code>-static-pie</code> on musl-libc (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17316">#17316</a>)</li>
</ul>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/straight-shoota">@straight-shoota</a>, <a href="https://github.com/ysbaddaden">@ysbaddaden</a></em></p>



<ul>
  <li>Uppercase instance and class variables (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17160">#17160</a>)</li>
  <li><code>HTTP::Request#local_address</code> and <code>#remote_address</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17260">#17260</a>)</li>
  <li><code>LLVM::Type#size</code> and <code>#alignment</code> (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17494">#17494</a>)</li>
</ul>



<ul>
  <li>Support for LLVM 23.1 and 24.0 (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17432">#17432</a>)</li>
  <li>Updated markd 0.6.0 (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17269">#17269</a>)</li>
</ul>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/straight-shoota">@straight-shoota</a>, <a href="https://github.com/hertzdevil">@hertzdevil</a></em></p>



<p>Updated distribution-scripts (<a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal/issues/17178">#17178</a>) brings upgrades of package dependencies, docker base images etc.</p>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/straight-shoota">@straight-shoota</a></em></p>

<p>This is the first release featuring builds for <code>aarch64-windows-msvc</code>.</p>

<p><em>Thanks, <a href="https://github.com/hertzdevil">@hertzdevil</a></em></p>



<p>Crystal established a forward compatibility policy in <a href="https://github.com/crystal-lang/rfcs/blob/master/text/0030-forward-compatibility.md">RFC 30</a>: Building Crystal 1.22 requires a stage-0 compiler at 1.14 or higher.</p>

<hr>

<div>
  <p>Thanks
</p>
<p>We have been able to do all of this thanks to the continued support of
 <a href="https://www.84codes.com/">84codes</a> and all other <a href="https://crystal-lang.org/sponsors/">sponsors</a>. To
 maintain and increase the development pace, donations, and sponsorships are
 essential. <a href="https://opencollective.com/crystal-lang">OpenCollective</a> is
 available for that.</p>

<p>Reach out to <a href="mailto:crystal@manas.tech">crystal@manas.tech</a> if you’d like to
 become a direct sponsor or find other ways to support Crystal. We thank you in
 advance!</p>


  <p><a href="https://crystal-lang.org/sponsors#contribute">Contribute</a>
</p></div>


</div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[HNInternal: eMudhra to Exit Global SSL/TLS Certificate Business; (105 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50194909</link>
            <guid>50194909</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 13:17:29 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50194909">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><tbody><tr id="50195217"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195648"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195648" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195648&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Why brutal? Reads very reasonable to me, nothing personal and nothing about someone or feelings everything about the entity and its obligations.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195836"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195922"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195081"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195090"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="0"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="0"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195090" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195090&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>"will sharpen focus on... trust services other than SSL/TLS"</p><p>Is there some reputation left to salvage here that I'm not aware of? Who would trust <i>them</i>?</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195861"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195861" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195861&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>They probably have cash in the bank and want to try to put it into something that might make money.  An extreme pivot.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195413"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195533"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195669"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195856"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195501"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="2"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="80"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195501" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195501&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>The customers for certs are technical people capable of seeing past that, so let them change it every day if they want.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195731"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="3"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="120"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195731" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195731&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>No, the customers for commercial certs are <i>mid-level managers</i>, who believe paying thousands of dollars for something you can also get for free means you are getting a higher quality - or in this case even believe it is "more secure". Rebranding will <i>absolutely</i> work for that, especially when you market your untrusted root as "ISO-certified private dedicated cloud-based PKI-as-a-Service".</p><p>The <i>technical people</i> will just install a certbot and use the default Let's Encrypt config.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195270"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195270" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195270&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>If they wanted to continue to make money, they could: exit the business, wait a bit, change the company name, and re-enter the business. Where "change the company name" could mean starting an actual new company, but with the same people, offices, contacts (OK, small problem there) and so on.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195220"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="0"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="0"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195220" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195220&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>CA Forum, Apple, Google and Mozilla are to be blamed for just giving license to random no security background far away company.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195459"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195459" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195459&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Yes, its all very well someone here shouting about "the absolutely brutal question battery the Chrome team publicly submitted to eMudhra".</p><p>But in all honesty there are serious questions to be asked about:</p><p>a) The initial approval process for <i>ANY</i> CA</p><p>b) The ongoing monitoring process for <i>ANY</i> CA</p><p>The fact this CA was based in India ultimately has nothing to do with it if we are being completely honest here.</p><p>Clearly there is a fundamental problem with the overarching approval and monitoring process which means it could have happened anywhere. This time it just happened to be India.</p><p>Secondly, in direct reference to the <i>"far away company"</i> comment India does have quite strict controls on both certain technical things (speaking generally here, not specifically in relation to certs ... e.g. use of sat phones).   And in general it is quite difficult (read: almost impossible) to turn up in India and do business as an external company.  So there is probably a genuine need for an Indian company CA since not every body can/wants to use Let's Encrypt or whatever.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195860"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195545"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195864"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195864" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195864&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>As a reminder Mozilla was warned not to add the Chinese government as a ca they pushed back claiming lack of evidence and only removed them as a ca once cnnic got caught issuing certs for google domains (by accident )</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195455"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195455" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195455&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Not to give into conspiracy theories but this company might be ideologically even if not materially aligned to current regime in India. With right kind of nudge any kind business contract is possible without any competency.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195318"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195318" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195318&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Yes, not sure what the timeline was where trust was given to this company. Just horrible for the entire secure Internet. They've yet to answer many of the basic questions raised throughout this process too.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195320"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195787"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195787" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195787&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>If anyone else also struggles to understand all the "4" references in the comments: One of their private keys was "4", with others also being in the 2-3 digit range.</p><p>Apparently they generated them like this: <a href="https://xkcd.com/221/" rel="nofollow">https://xkcd.com/221/</a></p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195707"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195458"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="0"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="0"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195458" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195458&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>I think this opens bigger questions on who and how people get access to these capabilities. After that recent cold wallet physical supply chain take over, what sort of people are able to get certs for anything they need?</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195519"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195519" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195519&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>eMudhra is primarily used by the Indian government, so not hard to infer how they got into allowlists.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195481"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195782"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195762"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195418"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195868"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195528"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195267"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="0"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="0"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195267" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195267&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50194909"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Terrible company, such evasive statements. Seems ripe for disruption.</p><p>Surely they should be investigated by the local regulator.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195601"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195454"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195432"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195202"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195389"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195120"><td></td></tr></tbody></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
