            <title><![CDATA[Several Linux kernel vulnerabilities accidentally posted to public mailing list (238 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/LKML-New-Trend-Vulnerabilities</link>
            <guid>50193804</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 11:12:37 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/LKML-New-Trend-Vulnerabilities">https://www.phoronix.com/news/LKML-New-Trend-Vulnerabilities</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50193804">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
<p><img alt="LINUX SECURITY" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxsecurity.webp" width="100" height="100"></p><p>
Seemingly as an accident, overnight several new Linux kernel vulnerability reports were posted to the public Linux kernel mailing list rather than the private security mailing list. The vulnerabilities have yet to be patched on mainline but at least in some cases there are patches undergoing testing or queued in "-next" branches. The CVSS scores are as high as 9.0.
</p><p>
Trend Micro's Zero Day Initiative sent out a number of vulnerability reports to the public Linux kernel mailing list (LKML) rather than the typical private security mailing list. This seemingly was a mistake with Linux's second-in-command Greg Kroah-Hartman responding to each report asking about their submission to the public mailing list.
</p><p><img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=public_zdi" alt="Greg KH message"></p>
<p>Among these newly-disclosed vulnerabilities include:
</p><p>
<a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/BYAPR01MB3830472B5702C089B0BBB77D80662@BYAPR01MB3830.prod.exchangelabs.com/">ZDI-CAN-33549</a>  as a CVSS 9.0 scored vulnerability for SMB Direct remote code execution vulnerability due to integer underflow. For this one there is a patch in a Git branch of code intended for Linux v7.5 but now presumably will be expedited for getting to mainline.
</p><p>
<a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/BYAPR01MB383076AC412E19C48CFBEAE980662@BYAPR01MB3830.prod.exchangelabs.com/">ZDI-CAN-33774</a> as a KSMBD race condition that can lead to remote code execution. Another CVSS 9.0 rated vulnerability.
</p><p>
<a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/BYAPR01MB3830CECF5D0871064C59BA0280662@BYAPR01MB3830.prod.exchangelabs.com/">ZDI-CAN-33367</a> is a vulneravbility for skbuff skb_zerocopy in the network code that can lead to local privilege escalation. For this one Linux kernel developers received a security report from another source last week over the same issue and a patch is expected to be posted soon.
</p><p>
<a href="https://lore.kernel.org/dri-devel/2026102254-protector-seventy-6454@gregkh/">ZDI-CAN-34068</a> as a VMWGFX VMware graphics driver bug with an out-of-bounds write leading to local privilege escalation.
</p><p>
<a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/2026102226-duly-imprison-a2c1@gregkh/">ZDI-CAN-34076</a> an uninitialized resource in the NVMET code leading to information disclosure.
</p><p>
<a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/BYAPR01MB383046745372577D8F5902D580672@BYAPR01MB3830.prod.exchangelabs.com/">ZDI-CAN-32005</a> was also posted to the mailing list a day prior for a search-domain TCP query stack buffer overflow leading to local privilege escalation.
</p><p>
The bug and security reports keep on coming in the AI fueled world.</p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[HNInternal: Tell HN: I unknowingly broke the PHP RCE embargo. I am sorry. (322 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50193416</link>
            <guid>50193416</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 10:11:44 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50193416">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><tbody><tr id="50200322"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="0"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="0"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200322" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200322&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>if you received the information about the RCEs <i>without</i> the information about the embargo, then <i>you did not break the embargo</i>. someone else up the chain did.</p><p>this whole saga will be analyzed and we'll all come out better for it at the end.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200368"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200428"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="2"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="80"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200428" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200428&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>This is exactly how it works.  People who are part of the embargo are responsible for making sure that anyone they share information with -- which should usually be nobody! -- are aware of and abide by the embargo.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200413"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="2"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="80"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200413" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200413&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>you cannot be subject to an embargo of which you had no knowledge and took no participation in.</p><p>the embargo was <i>already broken</i> when pete received the information.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200514"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200479"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195162"><td></td></tr><tr id="50199396"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50199396" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50199396&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>What does “take full responsibility” mean in the present context? Are you forfeiting all your assets and pledging the next twenty generations of your descendants into peonage?</p><p>(For clarity, I’m being facetious, but it’s a really meaningless and empty phrase to commit to the written record.)</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200054"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="2"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="80"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200054" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200054&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>&gt;What does “take full responsibility” mean in the present context?</p><p>I posted something that -- in hindsight -- I shouldn't have, and it had repercussions for a lot of people. An action that I took had considerable knock-in effects around the world.</p><p>Some outlets are reporting that my action of posting something that I believed to be in good faith was an indicator or catalyst for the embargo to be tossed out and a previously-agreed schedule to change in short order.</p><p>That's not a normal Wednesday for me. Not even vaguely. I've not slept well since, and my taking ownership with an apology is this new course of action. Hopefully I'll sleep tonight.</p><p>re: assets and peonage – I am but a broke &amp; wizened nerd with no known descendants. If you're ever in Cornwall, UK: look me up, I'll gladly buy you a sleep-friendly beverage. And I’m not being facetious.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200277"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="3"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="120"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200277" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200277&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Shit happens.</p><p>Years ago I lost $250K at work, well gave away, so real money out the door. I figured out why we were bleeding, that the mistake was mine (2008 vs 2009 in a function) and then changed the pattern to use explicit names rather than numbered events.</p><p>I figured at the end of the day I was fired. My boss laughed at me and told me good job for owning my mistake, fixing it, and then making sure it could not happen again.</p><p>Honestly don't just apologize - tell the whole story, in full. Tell us what you should have done differently at each step. Tell us where the process failed you and us. Those sorts of narratives and suggestions are how processes get improved. There is a humble and informative post mortem that you can write up and publish (beyond HN) for everyone's edification. But that should honestly be a Monday thing, when you have a clear head and some more perspective on the whole situation.</p><p>Honestly though, dont beat yourself up over this.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200451"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200536"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="2"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="80"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200536" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200536&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>It does NOT mean "He agrees to settle any financial claims against himself or his employer, for damages caused by negligence".</p><p>Could have said "I blame only myself for this mistake, nothing else needs to change".</p><p>This will keep happening until the system where this can happen runs out of willing scapegoats.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200351"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="2"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="80"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200351" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200351&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>&gt; What does “take full responsibility” mean in the present context?</p><p>Aside from Pete's reply (see adjacent comment), I'd add that him taking full responsibility is also his way of saying his post was nobody else's fault but his.</p><p>In other words it's less about forfeiting assets and descendant peonage, and more about saying "the buck stops with me".</p><p>At least that's my read of his sentiment. Pete can expand if need be.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200390"><td></td></tr><tr id="50193835"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="0"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="0"></td><td><center><a id="up_50193835" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50193835&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Don't be silly, you can't possibly be responsible for breaking an embargo you weren't subject to or informed about.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50194000"><td></td></tr><tr id="50194712"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="2"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="80"></td><td><center><a id="up_50194712" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50194712&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Not sure what you are apologizing for. It's a severely overblown bug that ended up on the front page of New York Times and the Japan Prime Minister had to get involved too. The bug itself is non trivial to exploit from just public requests to a web server so I am not sure what the hysteria is about. It's no different from the other hundred or so CVEs I see being posted or dropped everyday.</p><p>It will be headlines on Hacker News regardless due to sheer sensationalism, I think most DevSecOps people would rather find out the news here first than via their boss calling them after reading the morning news.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195092"><td></td></tr><tr id="50197130"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="4"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="160"></td><td><center><a id="up_50197130" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50197130&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Well, consider that their old IT minister has never used a computer in his life, it's probably a good thing she's the one issuing the call to update.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200218"><td></td></tr><tr id="50194221"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50194221" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50194221&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Responsibility may include vetting your sources - is this person competent and dutiful, to actually know there's no embargo? - and researching the situation, and thinking several moves ahead, and other stuff.</p><p>Reality is that everyone has to make decisions in finite time, using the resources they actually have.</p><p>And I'd put a whole lot more responsibility further up that chain of contacts.  And question how many other people they might have blabbered to.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50194246"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="2"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="80"></td><td><center><a id="up_50194246" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50194246&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>&gt;And question how many other people they might have blabbered to.</p><p>100%. I'm concerned about the two-way street this might also have opened me up to with information I shared privately with this individual. Nothing significant or incriminating, but the professional relationship is concluded.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200311"><td></td></tr><tr id="50196080"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200526"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200148"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200260"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200202"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200190"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200538"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="0"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="0"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200538" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200538&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Can someone, with the benefit of hindsight, explain the embargo ?  Why isn't there a clause "if the vuln leaks, embargo is broken" . I still don't understand the fuss.</p><p>Everyone knew about the vuln well before the patches going live.  The embargo, at that point, made things worse.</p><p>In plain english, please.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50198993"><td></td></tr><tr id="50199161"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50199161" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50199161&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>The modern version:</p><p>You have sinned.</p><p>Your penance:</p><p>Three Hail Claude's, one Our GPT and an act of loop engineering.</p><p>Two minutes of silent prompting.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50199976"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200389"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200400"><td></td></tr><tr id="50195688"><td></td></tr><tr id="50193889"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="0"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="0"></td><td><center><a id="up_50193889" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50193889&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>It happens. Information cascades are messy, especially with multi-layered contacts</p><p>Thanks for owning it ,the best thing now is ensuring the patch details are clear once the official release drops. Moving forward is what matters</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195482"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="1"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="40"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195482" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195482&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>You’re setting a great example of how to be open and take responsibility, even when you’re not actually at fault. I really respect how you’ve handled this. You’ve also given the community a valuable reminder that some security disclosures may be under embargo, and that it’s worth having in mind before sharing.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50195988"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="0"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="0"></td><td><center><a id="up_50195988" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50195988&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Really hope you are not in the US. This kind of admission would be used against you in a civil trial, and lawyers love to sue for just about any reason under the sun.  Someone could drag you to court and you might be able to defend yourself, but at an extremely painful cost both financially and emotionally.  While I think that would be utter bullshit, that does seem to be the way this country operates now.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50196236"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200172"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="2"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="80"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200172" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200172&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>The parent is getting downvoted but he is absolutely right. Do not ever do what you just did. It makes you an easy target for a frivolous lawsuit in the US or in other countries because of the potential liabilities in your message, they are real. Even if you didn't do anything wrong, you are painting a target on your back, for no reason. I would ask HN to delete that thread directly.</p><p>You cannot foresee the financial/civil consequences of that RCE and some people might be looking for scapegoats in the future.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200236"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200261"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200547"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="5"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="200"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200547" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200547&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>Sure it does. Saying sorry sometimes <i>reduces</i> liability risk, which is quite counterintuitive at first.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200417"><td></td></tr><tr id="50200505"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="4"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="160"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200505" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200505&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>While possibly true in theory, in practice it's not. Courts and judges do not take kindly to frivolous or unsubstantiated claims, and any halfway good attorney would advise you to not waste your money and your time on a claim you are unlikely to win or be able to collect on.</p><p>People and companies do get sued with the full intention of a settlement and it never going to trial. But that only happens when the defendant has deep pockets or good insurance.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr><tr id="50200550"><td><table><tbody><tr><td indent="4"><img src="https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif" height="1" width="160"></td><td><center><a id="up_50200550" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/vote?id=50200550&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50193416"></a></center></td><td><br>
<div><p>This isn't a matter of transparency, was the OP legally bound not to disclose the RCE? That whole "embargo" was pure courtesy between the people involved. The OP committed no fault at first place and the exploit was already in the wild anyway. Whoever really wanted to exploit it before the patch could without using the OP as a source.</p><p>&gt; Anyone can sue anyone else for any reason.</p><p>And it's easier to have cause to sue when someone publish an "admission of guilt", which makes a lawsuit harder to dismiss thus costlier.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr></tbody></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Faster than earcut: triangulating 3.7M map polygons in 489 ms (189 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://globus.software/blog/2026/10/16/cutting-map-polygons/</link>
            <guid>50193170</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 09:31:18 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://globus.software/blog/2026/10/16/cutting-map-polygons/">https://globus.software/blog/2026/10/16/cutting-map-polygons/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50193170">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div id="__blog-post-container"><p>We have published the polygon triangulator of our map engine as a small library,
<a href="https://github.com/GLMap/earstrip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">earstrip</a>, under the ISC license, the same one
<a href="https://github.com/mapbox/earcut" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">earcut</a> uses. It is one C++ header, and a WebAssembly build on
<a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/earstrip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">npm</a> that takes the same arguments as earcut. In C++ it is 14% faster than
earcut.hpp, in the browser 1.4 to 2.1 times as fast as earcut.js, and the
<a href="https://globus.software/blog/2026/10/16/cutting-map-polygons/#in-the-browser">comparison</a> is near the end. This post is about how we got
there.</p>
<p>A GPU fills triangles and nothing else. A map, on the other hand, is mostly polygons: buildings, parks, parking
lots, the strip of grass between two tram tracks. Here are a few blocks of Śródmieście in Warsaw the way our
renderer reads them from a map file, :</p>
<figure><canvas role="img" aria-label="City blocks of Warsaw drawn as polygon outlines"></canvas></figure>
<p>Before any of this gets a fill, every outline has to be cut into
. Hover over a polygon, or tap it, to see what it turns into.
These blocks hold 331 polygons with 5,151 vertices, and they become 4,514 triangles.</p>
<p>Zoom level 13 of our offline map of the Masovian Voivodeship, the region around Warsaw, has 3,740,425 polygons.
On one core of an M4 Pro, the constrained Delaunay triangulator in our engine needs 2.93 seconds to cut all of
them. This October I replaced it with ear clipping. To write this post I rebuilt the ear clipper one idea at a
time, one commit per idea, and timed every commit on those same 3.7 million polygons, so each section below ends
with a number.</p>
<!-- -->
<h2 id="delaunay">Delaunay<a href="#delaunay" aria-label="Direct link to Delaunay" title="Direct link to Delaunay" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>A triangulation of a set of points is Delaunay when no point lies inside the circle through the three corners of
any triangle. Two triangles that share an edge are enough to see how the rule works. Drag the point D:</p>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 600 360" role="img" aria-label="Two triangles sharing an edge; drag point D"><circle cx="300" cy="200" r="130.04999038831184"></circle><polygon points="172,177 323,328 428,177"></polygon><polygon points="172,177 428,177 300,34"></polygon><line x1="172" y1="177" x2="428" y2="177"></line><g><path d="M 193.21320343559643 198.21320343559643 A 30 30 0 0 0 202 177"></path><text x="220.0417356905869" y="201.89953848298467" text-anchor="middle">45<!-- -->°</text></g><circle cx="172" cy="177" r="5"></circle><circle cx="323" cy="328" r="5"></circle><circle cx="428" cy="177" r="5"></circle><text x="150" y="183">A</text><text x="317" y="354">B</text><text x="442" y="183">C</text><g><circle cx="300" cy="34" r="24"></circle><circle cx="300" cy="34" r="8"></circle></g><text x="314" y="22">D</text></svg><p><span>Smallest angle 45°<!-- -->; with BD instead it would be 37°</span></p></figure>
<p>While D stays outside the dashed circle through A, B and C, the shared edge is AC.
 and the edge flips to BD;
 and it flips back. Each flip is a local repair,
and a triangulation where no edge wants to flip is Delaunay. Of all the ways to triangulate the same points, it
has the largest smallest angle. The <span>orange</span> arc marks the smallest angle of the two
triangles, and the line under the figure shows what the other diagonal would give: wherever you put D, the edge
the circle rule picks wins.</p>
<p>"Constrained" means that the polygon's own edges must stay edges of the triangulation, even where the circle rule
would flip them away. Our triangulator inserts the vertices one at a time. For each new point it walks across the
triangles built so far until it finds the one the point falls into, splits it, and flips edges around the new
point until the circle rule holds again. It also forces the polygon's edges in, and at the end it keeps only the
triangles that lie inside the polygon and outside its holes.</p>
<p>Well-shaped triangles matter when you interpolate something across them or run a simulation on them. A flat fill
doesn't care: a GPU fills a needle-thin triangle as correctly as a fat one. And most of the work is spent on
polygons that hardly need any. Almost half of the Masovian polygons, 1.8 million, have four vertices or fewer.
A triangle needs no cutting at all and a convex quad is two triangles, yet the Delaunay code spends 419 ms on
them, building each triangulation from scratch.</p>
<h2 id="ears">Ears<a href="#ears" aria-label="Direct link to Ears" title="Direct link to Ears" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>An ear of a polygon is a corner you can cut off with one straight cut. Take three consecutive vertices a, b and c.
If the corner at b is convex and no other part of the polygon reaches into the triangle abc, that triangle is an
ear. Cut it off, and what remains is a polygon with one vertex fewer. In 1975 Gary Meisters proved that every
simple polygon with more than three vertices has at least two ears that don't overlap. So there is always another
ear to cut, and a polygon with n vertices falls apart into n − 2 triangles.</p>
<p>Here is <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/258988073" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">one of the buildings</a> from the blocks above. Move the slider
to cut its ears one at a time, or :</p>
<figure><svg viewBox="3107.4 841.4 795.2 538.2" role="img" aria-label="A building outline with 0 of 25 ears cut"><polygon points="3834,883.9999999999999 3849,1069 3814,1073 3827,1163 3860,1166 3854,1337 3670,1320 3683,1182 3678,1161 3664,1146 3651,1139 3635,1138 3606,1139 3599,1137 3596,1131 3448,1150 3445,1158 3440,1161 3440,1163 3426,1166 3409,1171 3395,1182 3384,1199 3378,1219 3372,1292 3188,1275 3150,974.9999999999999" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><polygon points="3834,883.9999999999999 3150,974.9999999999999 3188,1275 3372,1292 3378,1219 3384,1199 3395,1182 3409,1171 3426,1166 3440,1163 3440,1161 3445,1158 3448,1150 3596,1131 3599,1137 3606,1139 3635,1138 3651,1139 3664,1146 3678,1161 3683,1182 3670,1320 3854,1337 3860,1166 3827,1163 3814,1073 3849,1069" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><polygon points="3834,883.9999999999999 3849,1069 3150,974.9999999999999" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><circle cx="3378" cy="1219" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3384" cy="1199" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3395" cy="1182" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3409" cy="1171" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3426" cy="1166" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3440" cy="1161" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3448" cy="1150" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3596" cy="1131" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3635" cy="1138" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3651" cy="1139" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3664" cy="1146" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3678" cy="1161" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3683" cy="1182" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3827" cy="1163" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="3814" cy="1073" r="11.36" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle></svg><p><span>0<!-- --> / <!-- -->25</span></p></figure>
<p>The <span>green</span> triangle is the next ear to go. The algorithm needs one test, "is this corner an
ear?", and the simplest version of it checks the corner and then every other vertex of the boundary. <code>cross</code> is
the 2×2 determinant found in every geometry codebase; it is positive when c lies to the left of the line from a
to b, so for a boundary that runs counter-clockwise a positive <code>cross(a, b, c)</code> means a convex corner:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code><p><span>static</span><span> </span><span>double</span><span> </span><span>cross</span><span>(</span><span>double</span><span> ax</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>double</span><span> ay</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>double</span><span> bx</span><span>,</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>                    </span><span>double</span><span> by</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>double</span><span> cx</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>double</span><span> cy</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>{</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>return</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>bx </span><span>-</span><span> ax</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>cy </span><span>-</span><span> ay</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>by </span><span>-</span><span> ay</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>cx </span><span>-</span><span> ax</span><span>)</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>}</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>bool</span><span> </span><span>isEar</span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span> v</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>const</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>{</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>const</span><span> Node </span><span>&amp;</span><span>b </span><span>=</span><span> _nodes</span><span>[</span><span>v</span><span>]</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>const</span><span> Node </span><span>&amp;</span><span>a </span><span>=</span><span> _nodes</span><span>[</span><span>b</span><span>.</span><span>prev</span><span>]</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>&amp;</span><span>c </span><span>=</span><span> _nodes</span><span>[</span><span>b</span><span>.</span><span>next</span><span>]</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>cross</span><span>(</span><span>a</span><span>,</span><span> b</span><span>,</span><span> c</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&lt;=</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>return</span><span> </span><span>false</span><span>;</span><span> </span><span>// turns the wrong way, or not at all</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>for</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span> w </span><span>=</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>next</span><span>;</span><span> w </span><span>!=</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>prev</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>         w </span><span>=</span><span> _nodes</span><span>[</span><span>w</span><span>]</span><span>.</span><span>next</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>const</span><span> Node </span><span>&amp;</span><span>p </span><span>=</span><span> _nodes</span><span>[</span><span>w</span><span>]</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>cross</span><span>(</span><span>a</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&lt;</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span> </span><span>||</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>cross</span><span>(</span><span>b</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&lt;</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span> </span><span>||</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>cross</span><span>(</span><span>c</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&lt;</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>continue</span><span>;</span><span> </span><span>// outside the triangle</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>!</span><span>samePos</span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>,</span><span> a</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&amp;&amp;</span><span> </span><span>!</span><span>samePos</span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>,</span><span> b</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&amp;&amp;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>!</span><span>samePos</span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>,</span><span> c</span><span>)</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>return</span><span> </span><span>false</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>}</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>return</span><span> </span><span>true</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>}</span><br></p></code></pre></div>
<p>A point on an edge of the triangle counts as inside: if the ear touched the rest of the boundary, cutting it off
would leave a polygon that touches itself. The one exception is a point at exactly the position of a, b or c,
which happens where an outline meets itself at a single vertex. Holes make that common.</p>
<h2 id="holes">Holes<a href="#holes" aria-label="Direct link to Holes" title="Direct link to Holes" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>Ear clipping works on one closed boundary, and 11,345 of the Masovian polygons have holes, such as courtyards and
clearings in forests. The way to deal with them is to cut each hole open and stitch it to the outer ring with a
bridge, an edge from a vertex of the hole to a vertex of the boundary, walked once in each direction. The
boundary then runs around the outside, crosses the bridge, goes around the hole the other way and comes back.
There is no area between the two copies of the bridge, so the polygon is the same, but now it is one ring.</p>
<p>The bridge must not cross anything. David Eberly's <a href="https://www.geometrictools.com/Documentation/TriangulationByEarClipping.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">write-up of ear
clipping</a> has a neat way to find
one. Take the rightmost vertex of the hole, m, and cast a ray to the right. The first edge the ray hits, at I,
has an end P further to the right. If no boundary vertex lies inside the triangle m, I, P, the bridge m–P is
safe. You can drag the hole around:</p>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" role="img" aria-label="A polygon with one hole; drag the hole"><path d="M 6,92 L 46,92 L 56,56 L 66,92 L 94,92 L 94,8 L 6,8 Z M 16,70 L 32,67 L 34,53 L 19,55 Z" fill-rule="evenodd" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></path><polygon points="16,70 32,67 34,53 19,55" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><polygon points="34,53 94,53 94,92" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><line x1="34" y1="53" x2="94" y2="53" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></line><line x1="34" y1="53" x2="56" y2="56" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></line><circle cx="56" cy="56" r="2.2" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="94" cy="53" r="1"></circle><text x="95.8" y="58">I</text><text x="89" y="90">P</text><circle cx="34" cy="53" r="1.3"></circle><text x="28.5" y="51.5">m</text><polygon points="16,70 32,67 34,53 19,55"></polygon></svg><p><span>The tip of the dent lies in the triangle m, I, P. A bridge to P would cross the boundary, so it goes to the dent.</span></p></figure>
<p> the triangle is empty and the bridge goes to P.
 the <span>tip of the dent</span> sits
inside the triangle, and the bridge goes to the dent instead: of the dents in the triangle, Eberly takes the one
closest in angle to the ray. Holes are bridged rightmost first, so a hole further left can bridge to one that is
already part of the boundary.</p>
<p>Map data adds two cases of its own. Our coordinates are integers on a tile grid, so a ray along a grid line runs
 all the time; the crossing test passes
over such a vertex, and my first version got exactly this case wrong. And mappers often draw a courtyard that
 at one point. Such a hole needs no bridge:
the two rings are joined at the shared vertex.</p>
<h2 id="the-naive-clipper">The naive clipper (−1,812 ms)<a href="#the-naive-clipper" aria-label="Direct link to The naive clipper (−1,812 ms)" title="Direct link to The naive clipper (−1,812 ms)" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>With holes bridged, the first complete version walks around the boundary, tests corners with the <code>isEar</code> above,
cuts every ear it finds and writes each triangle as three indices. On the Masovian polygons it takes 1.12
seconds, 2.6 times faster than Delaunay. The chart splits each time by polygon size; hover over a part of a bar
to see its number, or click a row:</p>
<figure><div role="img" aria-label="Triangulation time of every step, split by polygon size"><p><span>Delaunay</span><span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span>2,931<!-- --> ms</span></span></p><p><span>1. Naive ears</span><span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span>1,119<!-- --> ms</span></span></p><p><span>2. Only dents</span><span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span>882<!-- --> ms</span></span></p><p><span>3. Convex shortcut</span><span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span>880<!-- --> ms</span></span></p><p><span>4. Zig-zag strips</span><span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span>762<!-- --> ms</span></span></p><p><span>5. Buckets</span><span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span>697<!-- --> ms</span></span></p><p><span>6. No branches</span><span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span>519<!-- --> ms</span></span></p><p><span>7. Marked hits</span><span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span>489<!-- --> ms</span></span></p></div><p><span><i></i>up to 4<!-- --> vertices</span><span><i></i>5–8<!-- --> vertices</span><span><i></i>9–32<!-- --> vertices</span><span><i></i>33–256<!-- --> vertices</span><span><i></i>257 and more<!-- --> vertices</span></p></figure>
<p> is shorter than
 in every size class but the last. The 1,123 largest polygons,
with 257 vertices or more, take 199 ms to clip and 90 ms with Delaunay. Each ear test there walks hundreds of
vertices, and a polygon needs hundreds of tests, so the cost grows with the square of its size. The rest of this
post is about making the ear test cheaper and needed less often.</p>
<h2 id="only-dents-can-block">Test only the dents (−237 ms)<a href="#only-dents-can-block" aria-label="Direct link to Test only the dents (−237 ms)" title="Direct link to Test only the dents (−237 ms)" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>Look at which vertices can actually stop a convex corner from being an ear. Say some vertices lie inside its
triangle, and take the one closest to the corner itself. Its two edges can't cross the sides of the triangle, so
both run back, away from the corner, and the polygon wraps around that vertex from the corner's side. That makes
it a reflex vertex, a <span>dent</span>. So if anything is inside the triangle, a dent is inside it
too, and the test only has to look at the dents.</p>
<p>The engine keeps a list of them. A dent leaves the list when cutting its neighbour makes it convex, and a convex
corner never turns into a dent, so the list only shrinks. The test becomes:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code><p><span>bool</span><span> </span><span>isEar</span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span> v</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>const</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>{</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>const</span><span> Node </span><span>&amp;</span><span>b </span><span>=</span><span> _nodes</span><span>[</span><span>v</span><span>]</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>b</span><span>.</span><span>reflex</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>return</span><span> </span><span>false</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>const</span><span> Node </span><span>&amp;</span><span>a </span><span>=</span><span> _nodes</span><span>[</span><span>b</span><span>.</span><span>prev</span><span>]</span><span>,</span><span> </span><span>&amp;</span><span>c </span><span>=</span><span> _nodes</span><span>[</span><span>b</span><span>.</span><span>next</span><span>]</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>for</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span> i </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>;</span><span> i </span><span>&lt;</span><span> _live</span><span>;</span><span> </span><span>++</span><span>i</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>const</span><span> Item </span><span>&amp;</span><span>p </span><span>=</span><span> _items</span><span>[</span><span>i</span><span>]</span><span>;</span><span> </span><span>// a dent</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>node </span><span>==</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>prev </span><span>||</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>node </span><span>==</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>next</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>continue</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>cross</span><span>(</span><span>a</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&lt;</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span> </span><span>||</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>cross</span><span>(</span><span>b</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&lt;</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span> </span><span>||</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>cross</span><span>(</span><span>c</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&lt;</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>continue</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>!=</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>||</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>y </span><span>!=</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&amp;&amp;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>!=</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>||</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>y </span><span>!=</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&amp;&amp;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>!=</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>||</span><span> p</span><span>.</span><span>y </span><span>!=</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>            </span><span>return</span><span> </span><span>false</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>}</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>return</span><span> </span><span>true</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>}</span><br></p></code></pre></div>
<p>The 28.1 million ear tests now look at 185 million points instead of 353 million, and the total drops to
. That is less than you might expect, because building outlines
have a lot of dents.
The building with the arch above has 15 of them among its 27 vertices. And the smallest polygons get slower, from
34 to 54 ms: for a triangle or a quad, sorting the corners into dents and the rest costs more than the walk it
saves.</p>
<h2 id="polygons-that-need-no-tests">Skip tests on convex polygons (−2 ms)<a href="#polygons-that-need-no-tests" aria-label="Direct link to Skip tests on convex polygons (−2 ms)" title="Direct link to Skip tests on convex polygons (−2 ms)" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>A polygon without dents is convex, and every corner of a convex polygon is an ear, so it can be cut without a
single test. Checking for that needs care. "Every corner turns left" is not enough:</p>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" role="img" aria-label="A five-pointed star"><polygon points="50,8 25.313019403716122,83.97871376374779 89.94437368439645,37.02128623625222 10.055626315603554,37.02128623625221 74.68698059628386,83.9787137637478" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><path d="M 2.6 0 L -2 -2 L -2 2 Z" transform="translate(37.65650970185806 45.989356881873896) rotate(108.00000000000001)"></path><path d="M 2.6 0 L -2 -2 L -2 2 Z" transform="translate(57.62869654405629 60.50000000000001) rotate(-35.999999999999986)"></path><path d="M 2.6 0 L -2 -2 L -2 2 Z" transform="translate(50 37.021286236252216) rotate(-180)"></path><path d="M 2.6 0 L -2 -2 L -2 2 Z" transform="translate(42.3713034559437 60.5) rotate(36.000000000000014)"></path><path d="M 2.6 0 L -2 -2 L -2 2 Z" transform="translate(62.34349029814193 45.9893568818739) rotate(-108)"></path><circle cx="25.313019403716122" cy="83.97871376374779" r="4" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="89.94437368439645" cy="37.02128623625222" r="4" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="10.055626315603554" cy="37.02128623625221" r="4" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle><circle cx="74.68698059628386" cy="83.9787137637478" r="4" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></circle></svg><div><p><span>Corners turning left: 5 of 5. Changes of x direction: 4. Not convex.</span></p></div></figure>
<p>The  turns left at all five corners, because it goes around its
centre twice. The engine adds one more condition. Walking around a convex ring, the edges go right along the
bottom and left along the top, so their x direction changes exactly twice, at the leftmost and the rightmost
point. The  passes; the star changes direction four times, at
the <span>circled</span> corners, and goes to the ordinary ear tests. Both counts come out of the same
loop that marks the dents, so the check costs almost nothing. Triangles and quads get their own shortcut before
that loop.</p>
<p>The shortcut removes 4.4 million of the 28.1 million ear tests, but those were the cheapest ones: a ring without
dents has nothing to scan. On its own it barely moves the total, ,
though the polygons with four vertices or fewer win back part of what the dents cost them, from 54 to 48 ms. It
matters more in the next step, where a convex ring becomes a single strip.</p>
<h2 id="strips">Write strips (−118 ms)<a href="#strips" aria-label="Direct link to Write strips (−118 ms)" title="Direct link to Write strips (−118 ms)" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>So far every triangle costs three indices. A GPU can also draw a triangle strip, where each new index adds a
triangle made of it and the two before. A convex ring makes one strip if you zig-zag across it:</p>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" role="img" aria-label="An octagon with 6 of 6 strip triangles"><polygon points="33.92729584066623,88.80294036547404 66.07270415933377,88.80294036547404 88.80294036547404,66.07270415933377 88.80294036547404,33.92729584066623 66.07270415933377,11.197059634525957 33.92729584066623,11.197059634525957 11.197059634525957,33.92729584066623 11.19705963452595,66.07270415933377" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><polygon points="33.92729584066623,88.80294036547404 66.07270415933377,88.80294036547404 11.19705963452595,66.07270415933377" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><polygon points="66.07270415933377,88.80294036547404 11.19705963452595,66.07270415933377 88.80294036547404,66.07270415933377" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><polygon points="11.19705963452595,66.07270415933377 88.80294036547404,66.07270415933377 11.197059634525957,33.92729584066623" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><polygon points="88.80294036547404,66.07270415933377 11.197059634525957,33.92729584066623 88.80294036547404,33.92729584066623" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><polygon points="11.197059634525957,33.92729584066623 88.80294036547404,33.92729584066623 33.92729584066623,11.197059634525957" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><polygon points="88.80294036547404,33.92729584066623 33.92729584066623,11.197059634525957 66.07270415933377,11.197059634525957" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polygon><polyline points="33.92729584066623,88.80294036547404 66.07270415933377,88.80294036547404 11.19705963452595,66.07270415933377 88.80294036547404,66.07270415933377 11.197059634525957,33.92729584066623 88.80294036547404,33.92729584066623 33.92729584066623,11.197059634525957 66.07270415933377,11.197059634525957" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></polyline><text x="31.837844299952845" y="95.84732261298566" text-anchor="middle">0</text><text x="68.16215570004715" y="95.84732261298566" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text x="93.84732261298566" y="70.16215570004715" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text x="93.84732261298566" y="33.83784429995285" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="68.16215570004715" y="8.152677387014336" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="31.837844299952845" y="8.152677387014336" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text x="6.152677387014336" y="33.83784429995285" text-anchor="middle">6</text><text x="6.152677387014329" y="70.16215570004715" text-anchor="middle">7</text></svg><p><code><span>0<!-- --> </span><span>1<!-- --> </span><span>7<!-- --> </span><span>2<!-- --> </span><span>6<!-- --> </span><span>3<!-- --> </span><span>5<!-- --> </span><span>4<!-- --> </span></code></p></figure>
<p>The ear clipper can write strips too. Cutting an ear makes the corners of its two neighbours sharper, so they
are good candidates for the next ear, and cutting one of them extends the strip by a single index. The engine
tries them first and keeps alternating between the two ends while the ears last. In its code the neighbours of
the cut vertex are a and b:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code><p><span>// The next ear alternates between the ends of the cut.</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>while</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>true</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>sideA</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>const</span><span> </span><span>uint32_t</span><span> z </span><span>=</span><span> n</span><span>[</span><span>a</span><span>]</span><span>.</span><span>prev</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        _strips</span><span>.</span><span>push_back</span><span>(</span><span>n</span><span>[</span><span>z</span><span>]</span><span>.</span><span>index</span><span>)</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>unlink</span><span>(</span><span>a</span><span>)</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        a </span><span>=</span><span> z</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>}</span><span> </span><span>else</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>const</span><span> </span><span>uint32_t</span><span> z </span><span>=</span><span> n</span><span>[</span><span>b</span><span>]</span><span>.</span><span>next</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        _strips</span><span>.</span><span>push_back</span><span>(</span><span>n</span><span>[</span><span>z</span><span>]</span><span>.</span><span>index</span><span>)</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>unlink</span><span>(</span><span>b</span><span>)</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        b </span><span>=</span><span> z</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>}</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    count</span><span>--</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    sideA </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>!</span><span>sideA</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>count </span><span>&lt;</span><span> </span><span>3</span><span> </span><span>||</span><span> </span><span>!</span><span>isEar</span><span>(</span><span>sideA </span><span>?</span><span> a </span><span>:</span><span> b</span><span>)</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        </span><span>break</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>}</span><br></p></code></pre></div>
<p>When the next corner is not an ear, the strip ends and the walk goes on from where it stopped. The 21.5 million
triangles now take 35.8 million indices instead of 64.5 million. The ear tests don't get fewer: the naive walk
already went on at the next corner after a cut, and now every strip also ends on a test that fails, 25.0 million
instead of 23.7 million. Still, the total falls to , and the
largest polygons drop from 146 to 103 ms.</p>
<h2 id="buckets">Sort the dents into buckets (−65 ms)<a href="#buckets" aria-label="Direct link to Sort the dents into buckets (−65 ms)" title="Direct link to Sort the dents into buckets (−65 ms)" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>A forest or a lake can have thousands of vertices and hundreds of dents, and each ear test still reads every one
of them. When a polygon has more than 32 dents, the engine sorts them into buckets along the longer side of their
bounding box, about two dents per bucket. An ear test looks only at the buckets that its triangle spans. As dents
turn convex and leave, the index is rebuilt once half of it is gone.</p>
<p>Points scanned fall from 181 million to 101 million, almost all of it on the large polygons: the polygons with
257 vertices or more go from 103 to 57 ms. Total: .</p>
<h2 id="counting-without-branches">Count without branches (−178 ms)<a href="#counting-without-branches" aria-label="Direct link to Count without branches (−178 ms)" title="Direct link to Count without branches (−178 ms)" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>The loop in the dent test exits at the first dent inside the triangle. Most of the time there is none: the test
reads all the dents and says yes. An early exit then buys nothing, and the three comparisons per point are
branches the CPU has to predict. The engine first runs a pass that only counts dents inside the triangle, with no
branches at all:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code><p><span>const</span><span> </span><span>double</span><span> abx </span><span>=</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>-</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> aby </span><span>=</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>y </span><span>-</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>const</span><span> </span><span>double</span><span> bcx </span><span>=</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>-</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> bcy </span><span>=</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>y </span><span>-</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>const</span><span> </span><span>double</span><span> cax </span><span>=</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>-</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>,</span><span> cay </span><span>=</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>y </span><span>-</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>uint32_t</span><span> hits </span><span>=</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>for</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span> i </span><span>=</span><span> begin</span><span>;</span><span> i </span><span>&lt;</span><span> end</span><span>;</span><span> </span><span>++</span><span>i</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>const</span><span> Item </span><span>&amp;</span><span>p </span><span>=</span><span> items</span><span>[</span><span>i</span><span>]</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>const</span><span> </span><span>double</span><span> side </span><span>=</span><span> std</span><span>::</span><span>min</span><span>(</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        std</span><span>::</span><span>min</span><span>(</span><span>abx </span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>y </span><span>-</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span> aby </span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>-</span><span> a</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>)</span><span>,</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>                 bcx </span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>y </span><span>-</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span> bcy </span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>-</span><span> b</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>)</span><span>)</span><span>,</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>        cax </span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>y </span><span>-</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>y</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>-</span><span> cay </span><span>*</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>x </span><span>-</span><span> c</span><span>.</span><span>x</span><span>)</span><span>)</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    hits </span><span>+=</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span>)</span><span>(</span><span>side </span><span>&gt;=</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&amp;</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span>)</span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>node </span><span>!=</span><span> ia</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>          </span><span>&amp;</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span>)</span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>node </span><span>!=</span><span> ic</span><span>)</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>}</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>if</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>hits </span><span>==</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>return</span><span> </span><span>true</span><span>;</span><br></p></code></pre></div>
<p>The smallest of the three determinants is non-negative exactly when the point is inside the closed triangle. Only
when the count is not zero does the exact loop from before run, to rule out dents that sit on a corner. The
bucket version does the same with a bounding-box test, 64 points at a time into a bit mask. The total falls to
, and the middle sizes gain the most: polygons with 33 to 256
vertices go from 327 to 216 ms.</p>
<h2 id="marked-dents">Test only the marked dents (−30 ms)<a href="#marked-dents" aria-label="Direct link to Test only the marked dents (−30 ms)" title="Direct link to Test only the marked dents (−30 ms)" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>When the count is not zero, the exact loop reads all the dents again, although the first pass has already seen
which ones were inside. The bucket version keeps that in its bit mask. The short list, up to 32 dents, can do the
same: the first pass sets one bit per hit instead of counting, and the exact test looks only at those dents:</p>
<div><pre tabindex="0"><code><p><span>    hits </span><span>|=</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span>)</span><span>(</span><span>side </span><span>&gt;=</span><span> </span><span>0</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&amp;</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span>)</span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>node </span><span>!=</span><span> ia</span><span>)</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>             </span><span>&amp;</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>uint32_t</span><span>)</span><span>(</span><span>p</span><span>.</span><span>node </span><span>!=</span><span> ic</span><span>)</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>&lt;&lt;</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>i </span><span>-</span><span> begin</span><span>)</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>}</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>while</span><span> </span><span>(</span><span>hits</span><span>)</span><span> </span><span>{</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>const</span><span> Item </span><span>&amp;</span><span>p </span><span>=</span><span> items</span><span>[</span><span>begin </span><span>+</span><span> </span><span>lowestBit</span><span>(</span><span>hits</span><span>)</span><span>]</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    hits </span><span>&amp;=</span><span> hits </span><span>-</span><span> </span><span>1</span><span>;</span><span></span><br></p><p><span>    </span><span>// the exact test from before</span><span></span><br></p><p><span></span><span>}</span><br></p></code></pre></div>
<p>Points read fall from 128 million to 120 million. This is the last step, and it is the code of earstrip as
published: , 6.0 times less than Delaunay on the same polygons.</p>
<h2 id="when-there-are-no-ears">When there are no ears<a href="#when-there-are-no-ears" aria-label="Direct link to When there are no ears" title="Direct link to When there are no ears" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>Meisters' theorem is about simple polygons. Map data is rounded to a tile grid, and rounding can break a simple
polygon. Here is the outline of <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/28010962" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Warszawa Śródmieście</a>, a railway
station less than a kilometre from the blocks above, rounded to grids of different size. The orange rings mark
vertices that land on the same point as their neighbour:</p>
<figure><canvas role="img" aria-label="The outline of a railway station rounded to a grid"></canvas><div><p><span>Grid step 73 cm. 68 of 154 vertices land on their neighbour. 84 triangles<!-- -->.</span></p></div></figure>
<p> per tile side, the grid our map files use, a grid step is 18
cm. Even there 31 of the 154 vertices merge with a neighbour, because mappers often place points a few centimetres
apart, and the outline survives.  nearly
half of the vertices merge, and the clipper still cuts it.
 the rounded outline touches itself in a way that leaves no ear
at all, and at 10 bits it happens to work again.</p>
<p>When the clipper walks all the way around the boundary without finding an ear, it drops the corners that have no
area and walks once more. If that fails too, it gives up, and the engine triangulates that polygon with the
Delaunay code, which still exists for this. In the Masovian data 76 polygons end up there. They come from a map
built before the last change on the other end: our map generator now rounds every polygon to the final grid with
GEOS's snap rounding and checks that ear clipping can cut it. In a fresh build of the Masovian map that repaired 273
polygons, and ear clipping can cut every polygon in the file.</p>
<p>Delaunay has one more job. Every hole needs its own bridge, and finding one scans the whole boundary, so a polygon
with thousands of holes pays for thousands of scans. Our world map has a polygon with 11,675 holes: ear clipping
takes 606 ms on it, Delaunay 17 ms. The engine now sends every polygon with 128 holes or more to Delaunay. In the
Masovian data that is two polygons.</p>
<p>The library keeps both of these fallbacks inside. earstrip covers a polygon with a constrained Delaunay
triangulation of its ring edges when the ears run out, when the triangles they leave cover the wrong area, and when
it has more than 128 rings; <code>run()</code> in C++ and <code>lastStatus()</code> in JavaScript say which way a polygon went. On the
56,472 polygons of our world map tiles, with up to 11,675 holes in one, earstrip takes 459 ms and earcut.hpp 2,892
ms. On the zoom 13 polygons that the ears can't cut exactly, earcut's triangles differ from an even-odd fill of the
rings on 31% of the pixels on average, earstrip's on 0.15%.</p>
<h2 id="in-the-browser">In the browser<a href="#in-the-browser" aria-label="Direct link to In the browser" title="Direct link to In the browser" translate="no">​</a></h2>
<p>Before the new clipper went into the app, I compared it with <a href="https://github.com/mapbox/earcut" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">earcut</a>, the ear
clipper behind MapLibre and Mapbox GL, in its C++ version earcut.hpp 3.2.4. Natively the two are close, and that
could have been the end of it. But we are working on a web renderer that runs the same C++ engine compiled to
WebAssembly, so I went further and compared the browser side too: both clippers compiled to WebAssembly, and
earcut.js 3.2.4, the JavaScript original that web maps run today. For that I took the clipper out of the engine
into earstrip. On all 5.8 million polygons of our test sets it writes exactly the engine's strips, and its
repository has these polygons and the code behind every number in this section. Its native benchmark is the one
behind the chart above, and the native row below is the last step run against earcut.hpp. Every WebAssembly build gives exactly the same triangles as its native version on all 4.4
million polygons of zoom levels 13 and 9. Milliseconds for the 3.74 million polygons of zoom 13, best of 7, one
thread, V8 in Node and JavaScriptCore in Bun:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>earstrip</th><th>earcut</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Native C++</td><td>489</td><td>557</td></tr><tr><td>WebAssembly, loop inside the module, V8 / JSC</td><td>601 / 566</td><td>930 / 843</td></tr><tr><td>WebAssembly, one call from JS per polygon, V8 / JSC</td><td>688 / 688</td><td>1,007 / 950</td></tr><tr><td>npm package, one call per polygon, V8 / JSC</td><td>766 / 807</td><td>1,103 / 1,290</td></tr><tr><td>npm package, direct calls, all polygons in one call, V8 / JSC</td><td>635 / 619</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Natively earcut.hpp is 14% slower than earstrip. Compiled to WebAssembly and run in V8, earstrip loses 23% and
earcut.hpp loses 67%, almost all of it in its main loop that searches for ears. It isn't the allocator: builds with
mimalloc and emmalloc give the same time. I haven't dug further. In V8, earcut.hpp in WebAssembly ends up only 19%
faster than plain earcut.js, and 10% once each polygon is a separate call from JavaScript.</p>
<p>The package rows are what a web page gets, and the earcut column there is earcut.js. The call that replaces
earcut takes the coordinates as JavaScript numbers and returns a new array of triangle indices every time: 1.4
times as fast as earcut.js in V8 and 1.6 times in JavaScriptCore. About half of what it loses against the loop
inside the module goes into copying the coordinates in and turning the strips into a list, the rest into calling
into WebAssembly once per polygon. The direct calls skip all of that: the
polygons go straight into the module's memory and come back as one index buffer of strips for WebGL 2 or WebGPU,
1.7 and 2.1 times as fast as earcut.js.</p>
<p>Some of the ideas above are in earcut too. It also relies on the fact that only a dent can block an ear, but it
checks that last, for every vertex that passes the bounding box and triangle tests, instead of keeping a list of
dents. Above 80 vertices it links them in z-order and scans only the stretch of that curve the triangle's bounding
box covers, which does the job of the buckets. What it doesn't have are the shortcut for convex polygons, the strips
and the count without branches: it writes three indices per triangle and stops at the first point inside.</p>
<p>earstrip is on <a href="https://github.com/GLMap/earstrip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a> and <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/earstrip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">npm</a>, with
the polygons from this post and the code to time it against earcut on your own machine.</p></div></div>]]></description>
