            <title><![CDATA[OpenAI CLI (221 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://github.com/openai/openai-cli</link>
            <guid>50191507</guid>
            <pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 05:23:11 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-cli">https://github.com/openai/openai-cli</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50191507">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-hpc="true" containertiming="hpc"><article itemprop="text">
<p dir="auto">The official CLI for the <a href="https://platform.openai.com/docs" rel="nofollow">OpenAI REST API</a>.</p>

<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Installation</h2><a id="user-content-installation" aria-label="Permalink: Installation" href="#installation"></a></p>
<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Installing with Homebrew</h3><a id="user-content-installing-with-homebrew" aria-label="Permalink: Installing with Homebrew" href="#installing-with-homebrew"></a></p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="brew install openai/tools/openai"><pre>brew install openai/tools/openai</pre></div>
<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Installing with Go</h3><a id="user-content-installing-with-go" aria-label="Permalink: Installing with Go" href="#installing-with-go"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">To test or install the CLI locally, you need <a href="https://go.dev/doc/install" rel="nofollow">Go</a> version 1.26 or later installed.</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="go install 'github.com/openai/openai-cli/cmd/openai@latest'"><pre>go install <span><span>'</span>github.com/openai/openai-cli/cmd/openai@latest<span>'</span></span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Once you have run <code>go install</code>, the binary is placed in your Go bin directory:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Default location</strong>: <code>$HOME/go/bin</code> (or <code>$GOPATH/bin</code> if GOPATH is set)</li>
<li><strong>Check your path</strong>: Run <code>go env GOPATH</code> to see the base directory</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto">If commands aren't found after installation, add the Go bin directory to your PATH:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="# Add to your shell profile (.zshrc, .bashrc, etc.)
export PATH=&quot;$PATH:$(go env GOPATH)/bin&quot;"><pre><span><span>#</span> Add to your shell profile (.zshrc, .bashrc, etc.)</span>
<span>export</span> PATH=<span><span>"</span><span>$PATH</span>:<span><span>$(</span>go env GOPATH<span>)</span></span>/bin<span>"</span></span></pre></div>

<p dir="auto">The first eligible interactive <code>openai</code> run quietly saves image picker Tab
shortcuts for future Bash, zsh, and fish sessions. Open a new terminal to use
them. See <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-cli/blob/main/docs/image-picker-shell-setup.md">shortcut setup and removal</a> for
requirements and opt-out. PowerShell keeps normal Tab completion; press Enter
after <code>openai images generate</code> to open the picker.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Update notices</h3><a id="user-content-update-notices" aria-label="Permalink: Update notices" href="#update-notices"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Bare <code>openai</code> checks for newer stable releases when stdin, stdout, and stderr are terminals.
It adds a plain-text notice below help, at most once every seven days.
Explicit help, commands, completion, CI, and redirected streams skip the check entirely.</p>
<p dir="auto">The notice includes an update command when the CLI can verify its installation method.
Verified Homebrew installations use <code>brew upgrade --cask openai/tools/openai</code>.
Go commands require a verified binary, an available Go command, and an explicit matching <code>GOBIN</code>.
Custom <code>GOOS</code> or <code>GOARCH</code> settings select the installation guide instead.
Go upgrades to major version 2 or later also select the installation guide.
Unknown installations receive the installation-guide link instead.
The CLI never installs updates automatically.</p>
<p dir="auto">Checks use public GitHub release metadata without API credentials, at most once daily.
Checks respect <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> and <code>NO_PROXY</code>, including their lowercase forms.
Update discovery waits at most 200 milliseconds. Failed checks remain silent.
Package-manager availability can lag a new GitHub release.
Set <code>OPENAI_CLI_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code> to disable checks and notices.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Running Locally</h3><a id="user-content-running-locally" aria-label="Permalink: Running Locally" href="#running-locally"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">After cloning the git repository for this project, you can use the
<code>scripts/run</code> script to run the tool locally:</p>

<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Usage</h2><a id="user-content-usage" aria-label="Permalink: Usage" href="#usage"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">The CLI follows a resource-based command structure:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="openai <resource> [subresource...] <command> [flags...]"><pre>openai <span>&lt;</span>resource<span>&gt;</span> [subresource...] <span>&lt;</span>command<span>&gt;</span> [flags...]</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Standard API endpoints require an <a href="https://platform.openai.com/settings/organization/api-keys" rel="nofollow">API key</a>:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="export OPENAI_API_KEY=&quot;sk-...&quot;

openai responses create \
  --input &quot;Say this is a test&quot; \
  --model gpt-5.5"><pre><span>export</span> OPENAI_API_KEY=<span><span>"</span>sk-...<span>"</span></span>

openai responses create \
  --input <span><span>"</span>Say this is a test<span>"</span></span> \
  --model gpt-5.5</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Admin endpoints require an <a href="https://platform.openai.com/settings/organization/admin-keys" rel="nofollow">admin API key</a>:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="export OPENAI_ADMIN_KEY=&quot;sk-admin-...&quot;

openai admin organization usage completions \
  --start-time 1735689600 \
  --end-time 1735776000 \
  --bucket-width 1d"><pre><span>export</span> OPENAI_ADMIN_KEY=<span><span>"</span>sk-admin-...<span>"</span></span>

openai admin organization usage completions \
  --start-time 1735689600 \
  --end-time 1735776000 \
  --bucket-width 1d</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Browse each group with <code>--help</code>, for example <code>openai admin --help</code> or
<code>openai admin organization --help</code>. Existing colon commands such as
<code>openai admin:organization:usage completions</code> continue to work.</p>
<p dir="auto">Run <code>openai</code> to browse command groups by task, with descriptions for each group.
Group help separates actions from nested command groups and lists every immediate command.
Help works without an API key or network connection:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="openai help setup                  # Enter your API key safely
openai images generate --help      # Example and every image option
openai help images generate        # The same complete image help
openai --help                      # Command groups and global options"><pre>openai <span>help</span> setup                  <span><span>#</span> Enter your API key safely</span>
openai images generate --help      <span><span>#</span> Example and every image option</span>
openai <span>help</span> images generate        <span><span>#</span> The same complete image help</span>
openai --help                      <span><span>#</span> Command groups and global options</span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Add <code>--help</code> (or <code>-h</code>) to any command for its complete help page.
<code>openai help COMMAND</code> shows the same page.
Command pages list global options. Use <code>openai --help</code> for their full descriptions.
You can place <code>--help</code> after valid command options.
The old <code>help --all</code> spelling prints migration guidance and the complete page.
Command behavior, defaults, and output formats remain unchanged.
Tab completion follows the same group order and supports help topics and file input paths.
After upgrading, <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-cli/blob/main/docs/shell-completion.md">reload shell completion</a> to enable the new file-path behavior.
Older loaded completion scripts keep their previous behavior until refreshed.</p>
<p dir="auto">Enable <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-cli/blob/main/docs/command-insertion.md">command insertion</a> to fill your command line
from examples, setup guidance, and recovery commands. Ctrl+X, then Tab searches suggestions
without running them. Double Tab is an optional binding for Bash, zsh, and fish.</p>
<p dir="auto">Successful JSON responses print readable text by default, including in pipes.
Use <code>--format json</code> or <code>--format jsonl</code> in scripts that parse API data.
See <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-cli/blob/main/docs/readable-output.md">reading command results</a> for extraction, binary
output, and the scope of readable presentation.</p>
<p dir="auto">Upload a local file with an explicit purpose, inspect its metadata, then download its contents:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="openai files upload &quot;upload space.txt&quot; --purpose user_data
openai files get file-example
openai files download file-example --output &quot;downloaded copy.txt&quot;
openai files download file-example > copy.txt"><pre>openai files upload <span><span>"</span>upload space.txt<span>"</span></span> --purpose user_data
openai files get file-example
openai files download file-example --output <span><span>"</span>downloaded copy.txt<span>"</span></span>
openai files download file-example <span>&gt;</span> copy.txt</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Replace <code>file-example</code> with the returned file ID. Upload takes a plain path, without an added <code>@</code>.
<code>create</code>, <code>retrieve</code>, <code>content</code>, and their existing flags remain available.
See <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-cli/blob/main/docs/files.md">working with files</a> for literal filenames, output formats, and script examples.</p>
<p dir="auto"><code>openai images generate --prompt "A tiny orange robot"</code> saves images to
<code>~/Downloads/gpt-images/</code> and prints their paths, including in pipes. The CLI
uses <code>gpt-image-2.5-sunburst</code> when saving without an explicit model or legacy
response format. Use <code>--model</code> for another exact ID, <code>--name</code> for a filename,
or <code>--output-dir</code> for an existing directory. Existing files are kept.
<code>--format json</code> returns API data without saving or applying the CLI preset.
<code>images edit</code> also saves new results while keeping source files.
<code>images create-variation</code> is retired and no longer available. To create a
variation, use <code>images edit</code> with a GPT Image model and a prompt. See
<a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-cli/blob/main/docs/image-generation-saving.md">image generation and saving</a> for commands
and defaults.</p>
<p dir="auto"><code>openai images preview "path/to/image.png"</code> redisplays a saved image without an
API request. Use <code>openai images inline on</code> or <code>off</code> to remember whether future
saved images appear automatically. Per-command <code>--inline auto</code>, <code>on</code> or <code>off</code>
overrides that setting. <code>on</code> also permits an image font in local Apple Terminal.</p>
<p dir="auto">Errors print actionable summaries to stderr by default. Use <code>--format-error json</code>
for original API error details, including unknown fields and SDK-reported stream
error events. For those events, extraction paths include the <code>error</code> envelope,
for example <code>--transform-error error.code</code>. Explicit <code>--format</code>
values <code>json</code>, <code>jsonl</code>, <code>raw</code>, and <code>yaml</code> also apply to errors unless overridden
by <code>--format-error</code>; an explicit <code>--format-error auto</code> or <code>text</code> restores summaries.
<code>--transform-error</code> extracts error data independently of successful output.
Malformed or empty error bodies receive a safe diagnostic; HTTP status is included
only when the SDK retains it. Summaries omit rejected values and request URLs;
explicit API error details can contain server-supplied sensitive data.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Environment variables</h3><a id="user-content-environment-variables" aria-label="Permalink: Environment variables" href="#environment-variables"></a></p>
